// Spoken answers (60-90 s) for kubernetes guide
(window.SPOKEN = window.SPOKEN || {})['kubernetes'] = {
  1: {
    a: `Pending means the scheduler hasn't placed the pod on a node, so I start with kubectl describe pod and read the Events section, because the scheduler almost always says why. The usual suspects are: CPU or memory requests larger than any node's allocatable capacity, a nodeSelector or affinity rule nothing satisfies, a taint with no matching toleration, or a PersistentVolumeClaim that isn't bound. I check kubectl describe nodes for allocated resources, and kubectl get pvc to see whether the claim is bound; a wrong storage class or a zone mismatch is very common. If it's pure capacity, I check whether the cluster autoscaler is enabled and why it hasn't scaled up, for example a node group at its maximum size. The fix follows the cause: right-size requests, add capacity, fix the selector or toleration, or fix the storage class. In production I'd also alert on pods Pending for more than five minutes, and set requests from real usage data so scheduling stays predictable.`,
    f: ["The pod fits on paper but still stays Pending. What else could it be?","How does the cluster autoscaler decide to add a node?"]
  },
  2: {
    a: `CrashLoopBackOff means the container starts, exits, and the kubelet keeps restarting it with an exponential backoff that caps at five minutes. First I find out why it exited: kubectl describe pod shows the last state, exit code, and reason, and kubectl logs with the previous flag shows the crashed container's output, since the current one may be empty. Exit code 1 usually means an application error or bad config, 137 means it was killed, often OOMKilled if the reason says so, and 139 is a segfault. Common causes are missing environment variables or secrets, a wrong command or entrypoint, an unreachable dependency at startup, and a liveness probe killing a slow-starting app. If it dies too fast to inspect, I override the command with sleep, or use kubectl debug with an ephemeral container. The fix is usually config, a resource limit, or a startup probe so liveness doesn't fire early. I'd also check recent deploys, because rolling back is the fastest fix while I investigate.`,
    f: ["The logs are empty. What now?","How do you tell an OOM kill from an application crash?"]
  },
  3: {
    a: `ImagePullBackOff means the kubelet tried to pull the image, failed, and is now backing off between retries. I start with kubectl describe pod and read the Events section, because it gives the exact error: manifest not found, unauthorized, or a timeout. Manifest not found usually means a typo in the image name or a tag that doesn't exist, so I verify it by pulling the image from a node or a workstation. Unauthorized points at credentials. For a private registry I check that the imagePullSecret exists in the pod's namespace, that it's referenced in the pod spec or attached to the service account, and that the credentials haven't expired. I can recreate it with kubectl create secret docker-registry. If the error is a timeout, I look at network egress, NAT, proxies, or a network policy blocking the registry. On GKE I'd prefer Workload Identity or the node service account with Artifact Registry reader rights rather than static secrets. Finally I check registry rate limits, especially on Docker Hub.`,
    f: ["Why might the same image pull fine on one node and fail on another?","How do you avoid Docker Hub rate limits in a cluster?"]
  },
  4: {
    a: `When a Deployment is stuck on the old version, I first run kubectl rollout status and kubectl describe deployment to see the conditions, then look at the new ReplicaSet's pods. The usual blockers are new pods in CrashLoopBackOff, a readiness probe that never passes, a resource quota or lack of capacity that keeps pods Pending, or a PodDisruptionBudget preventing old pods from being removed. The rollout is governed by maxUnavailable and maxSurge. If maxUnavailable is zero, a new pod must become Ready before an old one is terminated, so any failing new pod freezes the rollout, which is actually the safe behaviour. I read the pod events and logs, fix the cause, and if production is affected I run kubectl rollout undo to go back to the previous revision first and investigate after. I also check progressDeadlineSeconds, since the Deployment will report ProgressDeadlineExceeded after that time. In CI I'd make the pipeline wait on rollout status so a stuck rollout fails the deploy instead of passing silently.`,
    f: ["What do maxSurge and maxUnavailable control?","How would your pipeline detect and roll back a failed rollout automatically?"]
  },
  5: {
    a: `For a StatefulSet pod that won't start, the first thing to remember is that pods are created in order. Pod one will not start until pod zero is Running and Ready, so I look at the lowest-numbered pod that isn't healthy rather than the one that's reported. Then kubectl describe pod and the events. The most common cause is storage: each pod gets its own PersistentVolumeClaim, so I check kubectl get pvc and describe the claim. Is the StorageClass present, is the provisioner running, does the access mode match what the storage supports, and is there quota? With WaitForFirstConsumer, a zone mismatch between the scheduled node and the existing volume can leave a pod stuck, especially after a node moves. If the pod starts but never gets Ready, I check readiness probes and application logs, because the next replica waits on it. One important difference from Deployments: deleting a StatefulSet pod doesn't delete its PVC, which protects data, so if I need a clean slate I delete the PVC deliberately.`,
    f: ["Why can't a StatefulSet pod move to another zone with its volume?","What does podManagementPolicy Parallel change?"]
  },
  6: {
    a: `I start by confirming what's actually happening with kubectl top pod, sorted by CPU or memory, and compare it with the requests and limits in the spec. The two failure modes behave differently. Memory over the limit gets the container OOMKilled and restarted, which is loud. CPU over the limit gets throttled, which is silent: the pod just gets slow, so I check the container CPU throttled seconds metric in Prometheus. Immediate mitigation is to raise the limit or scale out replicas, depending on whether the load is legitimate. Then I find the cause: a leak shows as memory growing steadily, a traffic spike shows as load correlating with request rate, and a bad deploy shows as a step change at release time. Long term I'd set requests based on observed usage, run the Vertical Pod Autoscaler in recommendation mode to right-size them, and set an HPA for load-driven scaling. Requests drive scheduling, limits drive throttling and OOM, so both need to be realistic.`,
    f: ["How do you detect CPU throttling when usage looks low?","Should you set CPU limits at all?"]
  },
  7: {
    a: `I walk the path from the Service to the pod. First I look at the endpoints or EndpointSlices for the service. If they're empty, the selector doesn't match the pod labels, or the pods aren't Ready, and that's the classic cause. Second, I check that the targetPort matches the port the container actually listens on, and that the app binds to all interfaces, not just localhost. Third, readiness: a failing readiness probe removes a pod from endpoints on purpose. Then I test from inside the cluster with a debug pod: curl the service DNS name, then curl the pod IP directly. If the pod IP works but the service doesn't, I suspect kube-proxy or the service config. If DNS fails, it's CoreDNS. If neither works, I look at NetworkPolicies and the CNI. For external traffic I check the Ingress or load balancer layer separately. Most of the time it's a label typo or a port mismatch, so I check endpoints first, because it eliminates half the possibilities in thirty seconds.`,
    f: ["Endpoints are populated but requests still fail. Where next?","What does kube-proxy actually do in iptables or IPVS mode?"]
  },
  8: {
    a: `I start from inside a pod, because DNS problems are best tested from where the failure is. I exec in and run nslookup for kubernetes.default and for the failing service name, and look at /etc/resolv.conf to make sure it points to the cluster DNS service, usually the ten dot ninety-six dot zero dot ten address. Then I check CoreDNS itself: are the pods running in kube-system, what do their logs say, and is the Corefile in the ConfigMap valid? If DNS works for cluster names but not external ones, the upstream forwarders are suspect. If it works from some pods but not others, I think NetworkPolicy, because a default-deny policy blocks UDP and TCP 53 to CoreDNS unless explicitly allowed. That's a very common trap. Intermittent failures or five-second delays point at the ndots search path generating extra queries, or a conntrack race on UDP. Scaling CoreDNS, enabling NodeLocal DNSCache, and lowering ndots in the pod's dnsConfig are the usual fixes.`,
    f: ["Why do you sometimes see exactly five-second DNS delays in pods?","What does ndots do?"]
  },
  9: {
    a: `I debug Ingress in layers, from the outside in. First, is the Ingress controller running and does it have an external address? Then does the Ingress resource's ingressClassName match that controller, since a mismatch means nobody picks it up. Next, kubectl describe ingress shows the rules and the backend it resolved; I confirm the Service and port exist and that the Service has endpoints, which means pods are Ready. Then I look at the request itself: the host header and path must match a rule, otherwise you get the default backend and a 404. TLS adds another check: is the secret present and does the certificate cover the hostname? The controller logs are the most useful tool, because nginx or Envoy shows exactly which requests arrive and why they fail. A 502 or 503 usually means no healthy backend, and a 504 means the backend is too slow. On GKE the Ingress provisions a cloud load balancer, which can take several minutes, and I check backend health in the console since failing health checks mean readiness or path issues.`,
    f: ["What's the difference between a 502, 503 and 504 from the ingress?","How does Gateway API improve on Ingress?"]
  },
  10: {
    a: `When a NetworkPolicy is blocking traffic unexpectedly, I first remember the semantics. Pods are open by default, but as soon as any policy selects a pod for a direction, everything not explicitly allowed in that direction is denied. Allow rules are additive. So I list the policies in the namespace, read which ones select the source and destination pods, and check both sides: ingress rules on the destination and egress rules on the source. I test with a debug pod using nc or curl to the target IP and port, which also tells me if it's DNS or the connection itself. Egress to CoreDNS on port 53 is the classic thing people forget. To confirm a policy is the cause I can temporarily remove or loosen it in a non-production environment and add rules back one at a time. Also, I verify the CNI actually enforces NetworkPolicies, because with plain Flannel they're silently ignored. Cilium and Calico also give flow logs and policy verdicts, which make this far easier.`,
    f: ["How do you allow DNS in a default-deny namespace?","What tools show you which policy dropped a packet?"]
  },
  11: {
    a: `A PVC stuck in Pending means no volume has been bound to it. I run kubectl describe pvc and read the events, which usually state the reason directly. Common causes: there's no default StorageClass and the claim doesn't name one, the named StorageClass doesn't exist, or its provisioner isn't running, so I check the provisioner or CSI driver pods in kube-system. Another cause is an access mode the storage can't offer, for example ReadWriteMany on block storage that only supports ReadWriteOnce. Quota can also block it, either namespace storage quota or the cloud's disk quota. One important case isn't an error at all: with volumeBindingMode WaitForFirstConsumer the claim stays Pending until a pod using it is scheduled, because the system wants to pick the volume's zone based on where the pod lands. In that case I check why the pod isn't scheduling. Finally, if I'm using static provisioning, I check that a matching PersistentVolume exists with compatible size, access mode, and class.`,
    f: ["What's the difference between Immediate and WaitForFirstConsumer binding?","What happens to the data when a PVC is deleted?"]
  },
  12: {
    a: `If a pod lost its data after a restart, I first work out what kind of storage it was using. Describing the pod shows its volumes. The most common cause is that the application wrote to the container filesystem or to an emptyDir. The container filesystem is lost when the container is recreated, and an emptyDir survives container restarts but is destroyed when the pod is deleted or evicted. If a PVC was used, I check that it's still bound and what the PersistentVolume's reclaim policy is, because with Delete the underlying volume is removed when the claim is deleted. I also confirm the mountPath matches the directory where the application actually writes its data; a mismatch means the app is writing to ephemeral container storage while the volume sits empty next to it. For production stateful workloads I'd use a StatefulSet with PVCs and a Retain reclaim policy, keep tested backups or snapshots, and never rely on emptyDir for anything that must survive.`,
    f: ["What are the PV reclaim policies?","How do you back up persistent volumes in Kubernetes?"]
  },
  13: {
    a: `Native Kubernetes Secrets are only base64-encoded, not encrypted, so the real protections are access control and storage. The risks are: anyone with RBAC permission to get or list secrets in a namespace can read them, secrets live in etcd and are readable by anyone with etcd access unless encryption at rest is enabled, and they're easy to leak through environment variables, logs, or Git. My hardening is layered. Turn on etcd encryption at rest, ideally with a KMS provider. Tighten RBAC so only the workloads that need a secret can read it, and audit who can list secrets. Prefer mounting secrets as files over environment variables. Better still, keep the source of truth outside the cluster in a manager such as Vault or the cloud's secret manager, and sync it with External Secrets Operator or the Secrets Store CSI driver, which also gives rotation. And never commit secrets to Git; if GitOps requires it, use sealed or SOPS-encrypted secrets.`,
    f: ["Why are environment variables a weaker way to deliver secrets than files?","How do you rotate a secret without restarting everything?"]
  },
  14: {
    a: `To harden a pod that runs as root, I set a securityContext. runAsNonRoot true with a specific non-zero user, allowPrivilegeEscalation false, readOnlyRootFilesystem true with writable emptyDir mounts only where needed, and drop all Linux capabilities, adding back only what's required. I also set seccompProfile to RuntimeDefault and avoid privileged containers, hostPath mounts, and host networking. The image has to cooperate, so I build it with a non-root USER and fix file permissions. Then I enforce this cluster-wide rather than trusting each team: Pod Security Admission with the restricted profile enforced on production namespaces, or Kyverno or Gatekeeper policies where I need finer control. To audit what exists already, I query pod specs for their security contexts and run kube-bench for node and cluster checks. For runtime detection I add Falco, which alerts on things like a shell spawned in a container. The baseline profile blocks known escalations, while restricted follows current hardening best practice.`,
    f: ["What are the three Pod Security Standards profiles?","What breaks when you set readOnlyRootFilesystem?"]
  },
  15: {
    a: `To audit an over-privileged service account, I start by asking what it can actually do. kubectl auth can-i with the list flag and the as flag for the service account shows its effective permissions. Then I find where they come from by searching RoleBindings and ClusterRoleBindings that reference it, and describing the roles. Tools like rbac-tool or kubectl who-can help answer who can perform a sensitive verb across the cluster. The fix follows least privilege: replace ClusterRoleBindings with namespace-scoped RoleBindings where possible, give each workload its own dedicated service account instead of using default, remove wildcard verbs and resources, and never give cluster-admin to an application. I also disable token auto-mounting for pods that don't call the API. A common source of excess permissions is Helm charts that ship ClusterRoleBindings for convenience, so I review rendered chart RBAC before installing. Then I enforce it by policy and keep reviewing, because permissions drift over time.`,
    f: ["How do you find who can read secrets cluster-wide?","What is the risk of the default service account?"]
  },
  16: {
    a: `I treat privilege escalation as something to prevent at several layers, plus detect at runtime. At admission, I enforce Pod Security Admission restricted profile or Kyverno or Gatekeeper policies that reject privileged containers, host namespaces, hostPath mounts, added capabilities, and allowPrivilegeEscalation. At the identity layer, I keep RBAC tight and specifically watch dangerous permissions: the ability to create pods, exec into pods, read secrets, bind roles, or impersonate, since each can be a path to cluster-admin. At the network layer, NetworkPolicies limit lateral movement if a pod is compromised. At runtime, Falco watches syscalls and flags things like a shell in a container, writes under etc, or a privileged container starting. And I enable API server audit logging, because without it there's no forensic trail after an incident; it's often missed on self-managed clusters. Finally, I keep nodes and the runtime patched, since container escapes rely on kernel and runtime vulnerabilities.`,
    f: ["Which RBAC permissions are effectively equivalent to cluster-admin?","What would Falco alert on that admission control can't catch?"]
  },
  17: {
    a: `Kubernetes gives several placement tools, and I pick the simplest that does the job. nodeSelector matches node labels and is good for basic cases. Node affinity adds richer rules, with required rules that must be met and preferred rules that are best effort. Pod affinity and anti-affinity place pods relative to other pods, for example to co-locate a cache with an app or to keep replicas apart. Taints and tolerations work from the node side: a taint repels pods unless they tolerate it, which is how I reserve GPU or dedicated nodes. For high availability, I prefer topologySpreadConstraints to spread replicas evenly across zones and hosts, which is more predictable than anti-affinity at scale. If pods aren't landing where expected, I describe the pod and read the scheduler event, which lists exactly why each node was rejected. Remember that hard constraints can make pods unschedulable, so I use preferred rules when it's a nice-to-have, and always combine placement with PodDisruptionBudgets.`,
    f: ["When would you use topology spread constraints over anti-affinity?","How do taints differ from node affinity?"]
  },
  18: {
    a: `When a node comes under resource pressure, the kubelet starts evicting pods, and the order depends on QoS class. BestEffort pods, with no requests or limits, go first, then Burstable pods that exceed their requests, and Guaranteed pods, where requests equal limits, are last. So the key preventive step is setting accurate requests on everything, and setting requests equal to limits for critical workloads, especially memory. To investigate, I describe the node and read its conditions for MemoryPressure, DiskPressure, or PIDPressure, check top nodes, and list recently evicted pods. Disk pressure is often image layers or container logs, so I look at image garbage collection and log rotation. For voluntary disruptions like drains and upgrades, PodDisruptionBudgets limit how many replicas go down at once; they don't protect against involuntary eviction. I also set kube-reserved and system-reserved so the kubelet and OS have guaranteed headroom, and use the cluster autoscaler or Karpenter so capacity grows before pressure hits.`,
    f: ["How does the kubelet choose which pod to evict within a QoS class?","What's the difference between a PodDisruptionBudget and eviction thresholds?"]
  },
  19: {
    a: `When an HPA isn't scaling, I begin with kubectl describe hpa, which shows current metrics, targets, and conditions such as ScalingActive or AbleToScale, and often states the problem directly. Common causes: metrics-server isn't installed or healthy, so I check the metrics API service and run kubectl top pods; the pods have no resource requests, which makes utilization percentage impossible to compute; the target deployment's selector or reference is wrong; or the HPA is already at maxReplicas. Timing matters too: scale-down has a stabilization window of five minutes by default, and scale-up can be limited by behavior policies. If the pod can't actually be scheduled, new replicas stay Pending, which looks like scaling failed, and then the cluster autoscaler is the thing to check. For custom or external metrics, I verify the Prometheus Adapter or KEDA is working and the metric is registered. In production, I scale on a metric that reflects load, such as queue depth or request rate, rather than only CPU.`,
    f: ["Why does HPA need resource requests?","When would you choose KEDA over the standard HPA?"]
  },
  20: {
    a: `NotReady means the kubelet isn't reporting healthy to the API server, or the node reports a failing condition. I start with kubectl describe node and read Conditions and Events: it tells me whether it's MemoryPressure, DiskPressure, PIDPressure, or that the kubelet simply stopped posting status. Then I get onto the node if I can: kubelet status and its journal, the container runtime, disk space and inodes, memory, and connectivity to the API server. Frequent causes are a crashed or hung kubelet, a full disk, the container runtime failing, expired certificates, or the VM or its networking being down. Meanwhile the node controller taints the node, and after the default five minutes of toleration, pods are evicted and rescheduled; StatefulSet pods are handled carefully to avoid two copies running. If I can't fix it quickly, I cordon it, drain what I can, and replace the node, treating nodes as cattle, then root-cause from the logs. I'd also alert on NotReady nodes and kubelet health.`,
    f: ["Why do StatefulSet pods not move immediately?","How would you automate node replacement?"]
  },
  21: {
    a: `A pod in Running state only tells you the main process is alive, not that the application is working. I go through it systematically. First the logs, with follow and tail, to look for panics, repeated errors, or a stuck loop. Then check readiness: if the readiness probe is failing, the pod is removed from the Service endpoints and gets no traffic, which looks like the app not responding. Then I exec into the container and curl the app locally on its port, which separates an app problem from a network problem. If it works locally, the issue is the Service, selector, port, or network policy. If it hangs locally, the app may be deadlocked, out of threads or connections, or blocked on a dependency, so I check connections to the database and cache, resource usage, and thread dumps. I also verify the environment variables and ConfigMaps are what I expect. And I make sure liveness and readiness probes test the right thing, since a bad liveness probe can restart a healthy app.`,
    f: ["How do you tell whether the problem is the pod or the Service in front of it?","What should a good readiness probe check?"]
  },
  22: {
    a: `I build Kubernetes observability around the three pillars plus alerting. For metrics, kube-prometheus-stack gives Prometheus, Grafana, and the standard exporters like kube-state-metrics and node-exporter. For logs, a DaemonSet such as Fluent Bit ships container logs to Loki or Elasticsearch. For traces, applications emit OpenTelemetry to a collector and then to Tempo or Jaeger. Alertmanager routes alerts to paging. What matters more than the tools is what I alert on. I page on symptoms users feel, such as error rate and latency against SLOs, and use cause-level signals as dashboards or lower-urgency alerts: pod restart rates, pods stuck Pending, HPA stuck at max, PVC usage, node pressure, API server and etcd latency, and CoreDNS errors. Dashboards: a cluster overview, namespace resource usage, and per-service golden signals of latency, traffic, errors, and saturation. On a managed platform like GKE, Cloud Monitoring and managed Prometheus remove much of the operational burden. I also watch metric cardinality and log volume to control cost.`,
    f: ["What would you page on versus only put on a dashboard?","How do you control the cost of metrics and logs?"]
  },
  23: {
    a: `For a slow API server I look at its own metrics first: request duration by verb and resource, plus in-flight requests and priority and fairness queueing. That tells me whether the problem is broad or one hot resource. Then I look at the usual causes. etcd is the foundation, so I check etcd latency, disk fsync duration, and database size; slow disks are a classic cause. Next, expensive client behaviour: controllers or tools doing large unpaginated LIST calls or restarting watches create storms, and I can identify the culprit from audit logs and metrics by user agent. Then admission webhooks, because every request passes through them; a slow or unavailable webhook with a long timeout and failurePolicy Fail slows or blocks everything, so I check webhook latency metrics and set short timeouts and appropriate failure policies. I also check control plane node CPU and memory, and the object counts, like huge numbers of events or old Jobs. On managed services I can't touch etcd, so I focus on client behaviour and webhooks.`,
    f: ["How can an admission webhook take down a cluster?","What is API Priority and Fairness?"]
  },
  24: {
    a: `I upgrade one minor version at a time, control plane first, then nodes, respecting the version skew policy. Before starting I read the release notes, scan for deprecated or removed APIs with a tool like pluto or kubent, back up etcd, and confirm workloads have multiple replicas, PodDisruptionBudgets, and proper readiness probes. Then I upgrade the control plane; on managed services that's one operation, on kubeadm it's one control plane node at a time. For nodes I prefer a surge approach: add new nodes on the new version, cordon and drain old ones respecting PDBs, and verify each batch before continuing. For critical clusters I might build a parallel cluster and shift traffic instead. Zero downtime really depends on the workloads, not the cluster: a single replica or a missing PDB means drain causes downtime. I also upgrade add-ons like the CNI, CoreDNS, and ingress controller for compatibility, keep a rollback plan, and rehearse the whole thing in a lower environment first.`,
    f: ["A drain hangs forever. What is usually the cause?","How do you find deprecated APIs before upgrading?"]
  },
  25: {
    a: `etcd is the source of truth for the whole cluster, so the first question is whether it still has quorum. With three members you can lose one, with five you can lose two. If quorum is intact, I check member list and endpoint health, find the unhealthy member, and replace it by removing and re-adding it, so it syncs from the healthy ones. If quorum is lost, the cluster can't take writes. Then recovery means restoring from a snapshot: stop the API servers and etcd, restore the snapshot into a new data directory on each member with the new cluster configuration, start etcd, then bring up the API server and verify the nodes and workloads. The workloads already running keep running during this, since the kubelets and containers don't need etcd, but nothing can change. The prerequisite is automated, regular snapshots stored off the node in durable storage, and tested restores, because an untested backup is a hope, not a plan. On managed Kubernetes, the provider handles all of this.`,
    f: ["What happens to running pods while etcd is down?","How often would you snapshot etcd and how would you test the restore?"]
  },
  26: {
    a: `To drain a node without downtime I treat it as a controlled sequence. First I check that the cluster can absorb the work: other nodes need enough free capacity, and the workloads need more than one replica. Then I check PodDisruptionBudgets, because drain uses the Eviction API and will wait or hang if a PDB doesn't allow another pod to go, for example a single replica with minAvailable at one hundred percent. I cordon the node so nothing new schedules there, then run kubectl drain with ignore-daemonsets, and delete-emptydir-data if pods use emptyDir, and a sensible grace period so applications can shut down cleanly. Pods get evicted and rescheduled elsewhere, and I watch that they become Ready before moving on. After maintenance I uncordon the node. For production I'd do this during low traffic, drain one node at a time, and make sure applications handle SIGTERM and have preStop hooks so in-flight requests finish. Node pool upgrades on managed clusters follow the same logic automatically.`,
    f: ["What do you do when a drain hangs?","Why might a pod ignore SIGTERM and delay a drain?"]
  },
  27: {
    a: `On kubeadm clusters, control plane certificates expire after one year by default, and when they do the API server rejects connections: kubectl fails with TLS errors, and components can't talk to each other. I check the status with kubeadm certs check-expiration, which lists every certificate and its expiry. To renew, I run kubeadm certs renew all on each control plane node, then restart the static pod components, or move their manifests out and back, so they pick up the new certificates, and restart the kubelet. I also update the admin kubeconfig, because that certificate is embedded in the file. In an HA cluster I repeat this on every control plane node. Better than fixing an outage is preventing it: upgrading with kubeadm renews certificates automatically, I can alert on the x509 certificate expiry metric well ahead of time, and kubelet client certificate rotation should be enabled. On managed services like GKE or EKS, the provider rotates control plane certificates for you.`,
    f: ["Which certificates are not renewed automatically by kubeadm?","How would you monitor certificate expiry across the cluster?"]
  },
  28: {
    a: `GitOps means Git is the source of truth for cluster state, and a controller continuously reconciles the cluster toward it. With Argo CD, I create an Application that points to a repo and path and a destination cluster and namespace. Argo polls the repo, or receives a webhook for instant updates, renders the manifests from plain YAML, Helm, or Kustomize, compares them with live state, and reports whether the app is Synced and Healthy. With automated sync, prune, and self-heal enabled, changes merged to Git are applied automatically, resources removed from Git are deleted, and manual drift in the cluster is reverted. For many clusters or environments, I use ApplicationSets to generate Applications from a template. The practical benefits are an audit trail, easy rollback through git revert, and no cluster credentials in CI, since CI only builds images and updates a manifest. Details that matter: separating app code from config repos, handling secrets with sealed secrets or External Secrets, and using sync waves for ordering.`,
    f: ["How do you handle secrets in a GitOps workflow?","What does self-heal do and when is it dangerous?"]
  },
  29: {
    a: `Blue-green and canary are both ways to reduce deployment risk, with different trade-offs. In blue-green, I run the new version fully alongside the current one, test it, and then switch all traffic at once, in Kubernetes by changing the Service selector or the ingress target. Rollback is instant, just switch back, but it needs double the capacity during the switch. A canary sends a small percentage of real traffic to the new version, watches metrics, and increases gradually. With plain Kubernetes I can approximate it with two Deployments behind one Service, where replica counts set the ratio, but that's crude. For production I use Argo Rollouts or Flagger, which provide weighted steps through an ingress controller or service mesh and, importantly, automated analysis: they query Prometheus for error rate and latency and promote or roll back automatically. I choose canary when I want real-traffic validation with limited blast radius, and blue-green when I need fast, all-or-nothing cutover. Database changes must be backward compatible either way.`,
    f: ["How do database migrations fit into blue-green or canary?","What metrics would gate a canary promotion?"]
  },
  30: {
    a: `When a pipeline fails to deploy to Kubernetes, I split the problem into authentication, authorisation, artefact, and rollout. First, is the pipeline pointed at the right cluster and namespace? I print the current kubectl context. Then credentials: an expired token or kubeconfig is a frequent cause, so I check how the pipeline authenticates, ideally with short-lived OIDC federation instead of static secrets. Next RBAC: kubectl auth can-i with the pipeline's service account shows whether it may create or patch deployments and secrets. Then the artefact: has the image actually been pushed before apply runs, is the tag the one in the manifest, and can the cluster pull it? Race conditions between build and deploy are common. Then manifest problems: invalid YAML, wrong Helm values for the environment, or an admission webhook or policy rejecting the object. Finally, rollout: the pipeline should wait with kubectl rollout status and a timeout so a deployment that applies but never becomes healthy fails the job and can trigger rollback.`,
    f: ["How do you avoid long-lived cluster credentials in CI?","What should the pipeline do when rollout status times out?"]
  },
  31: {
    a: `To keep an application available during node maintenance, I use PodDisruptionBudgets together with sensible replica counts and spreading. A PDB declares either minAvailable or maxUnavailable for a set of pods, and the Eviction API honours it during voluntary disruptions like kubectl drain, node pool upgrades, and cluster autoscaler scale-down. So with six replicas and maxUnavailable of one, a drain evicts pods one at a time and waits for replacements to become Ready. It has limits: PDBs don't protect against involuntary events like a node crash or an OOM kill. And there's a trap, if minAvailable equals the number of replicas, or there's only one replica, then no pod can ever be evicted and the drain hangs. To make it actually work, I run at least two or three replicas, spread them across nodes and zones with topology spread constraints, make sure readiness probes are accurate so the budget counts only truly ready pods, and handle SIGTERM gracefully so connections drain.`,
    f: ["What happens when a PDB blocks a node upgrade?","What is the difference between voluntary and involuntary disruptions?"]
  },
  32: {
    a: `Multi-tenancy means several teams share a cluster safely, and I build it in layers. Each team or environment gets a namespace. On top of that, a ResourceQuota caps total CPU, memory, and object counts per namespace so one tenant can't starve others, and a LimitRange sets default requests and limits so pods don't run unbounded. RBAC scopes each team to its own namespaces. NetworkPolicies, starting with default deny, isolate traffic between tenants. Pod Security Admission or policy engines enforce a security baseline per namespace. Those layers give soft multi-tenancy, which is fine for teams that trust each other. For untrusted tenants I need harder isolation: dedicated node pools using taints and tolerations, sandboxed runtimes such as gVisor or Kata, or separate clusters or virtual clusters. I also add cost attribution through labels, self-service namespace creation with guardrails, and per-tenant observability. The trade-off is always efficiency versus blast radius: more sharing is cheaper, but a bad neighbour or an escape affects more people.`,
    f: ["When would you choose separate clusters over namespaces?","What does a ResourceQuota do that a LimitRange doesn't?"]
  },
  33: {
    a: `For high latency in a microservice system I work from the user-facing symptom down. First I establish where the time goes. With distributed tracing in Jaeger or Tempo I find which hop in the call chain is slow, and the golden signals per service tell me if it's latency alone or also errors and saturation. Once I have the slow service, I check its resources: CPU throttling is a classic hidden cause, visible in the throttled seconds metric even when CPU usage looks modest, along with memory pressure and garbage collection. Then the platform: DNS lookup latency, where the ndots setting multiplies queries; connection pool exhaustion; network policy or service mesh sidecar overhead; and noisy neighbours on the node. Then dependencies: the database, cache, or an external API, checking their latency and queueing. I look at percentiles, not averages, because tail latency hides in p99. And I correlate with recent deploys or traffic changes. Once the cause is found, I fix it and add an alert on that signal.`,
    f: ["How would you find CPU throttling when usage looks low?","What does a p99 spike with a flat p50 suggest?"]
  },
  34: {
    a: `I secure images in layers across the lifecycle rather than relying on one scan. In CI, I scan every image with a tool like Trivy and fail the build on critical vulnerabilities, and I also scan the Dockerfile and dependencies. I use minimal base images, such as distroless, to reduce the attack surface, and I pin images by digest rather than a mutable tag so what I tested is what runs. I sign images and generate an SBOM. In the registry, I enable continuous scanning, because new CVEs appear after an image is built, so re-scanning deployed images matters as much as the initial scan. At admission, I use Kyverno or Gatekeeper to allow only images from approved registries, with valid signatures, and optionally with a clean scan result. At runtime, Falco detects suspicious behaviour like a shell in a container or unexpected network activity. And I make sure there's a process for patching: rebuilding base images regularly and rolling out fixes, since scanning without remediation just creates a list.`,
    f: ["Why pin images by digest instead of tag?","How do you handle a critical CVE with no available fix?"]
  },
  35: {
    a: `I'd frame this as building a product for internal developers, not just running clusters. At the infrastructure layer, clusters are provisioned with Terraform and configured through GitOps with Argo CD, so everything is reproducible and auditable; I plan for multiple clusters or regions, with a defined upgrade cadence. The platform then offers golden paths: opinionated templates and Helm or Kustomize bases that include sensible defaults for probes, resources, autoscaling, and security, with self-service namespace creation and CI/CD integration. Guardrails come from policy as code, using Kyverno or Gatekeeper, plus quotas, network policies by default, and RBAC tied to the identity provider. Reliability: SLOs for the platform itself, observability built in so every team gets dashboards and alerts for free, and capacity planning with autoscaling. Governance and cost: labels for attribution, showback, and security scanning. As the owner, I'd measure success by developer outcomes such as time to first deploy and deployment frequency, run it like a product with documentation and a feedback loop, and avoid becoming a bottleneck by favouring self-service.`,
    f: ["How do you stop the platform team becoming a bottleneck?","How do you measure whether the platform is successful?"]
  },
  36: {
    a: `Both add containers to a pod, but their lifecycles differ. Init containers run sequentially to completion before the application containers start. If one fails, the pod restarts it, and the app doesn't start. They're right for one-time setup: waiting for a dependency to be reachable, running database migrations, fetching configuration or secrets, setting kernel parameters, or cloning content into a shared volume. Sidecars run alongside the main container for the whole life of the pod and extend or support it: log shippers, service-mesh proxies such as Envoy, metrics exporters, or credential refreshers. They share the pod's network namespace and volumes, so they communicate over localhost or shared files. A historical problem was sidecar ordering and Job completion, since a sidecar kept the pod from finishing; newer Kubernetes versions support native sidecars, which are init containers with restartPolicy Always, so they start before the app and stop after it. I'd use an init container when something must finish first, and a sidecar when something must keep running.`,
    f: ["How do native sidecars solve startup and shutdown ordering?","Why not run database migrations in an init container for every replica?"]
  },
  37: {
    a: `Requests are what the scheduler uses to place the pod, because it reserves that amount on a node, and they also set the guaranteed share under contention. Limits are the ceiling enforced at runtime, and they behave differently by resource. CPU is compressible: exceed the limit and the container is throttled through the CFS quota, which hurts latency but doesn't kill it. Memory is not compressible: exceed the limit and the container is OOMKilled. Requests and limits also determine the QoS class: Guaranteed when requests equal limits for every container, Burstable when some are set, and BestEffort when none are, and under node pressure BestEffort pods are evicted first, then Burstable. In practice I set memory requests equal to limits to avoid surprise kills, set CPU requests from observed usage like p90 or p95, and often leave CPU limits loose or off to avoid needless throttling, while LimitRanges and quotas keep namespaces sane. I use VPA in recommendation mode to tune the numbers from real data.`,
    f: ["Why can CPU throttling happen when usage looks low?","Would you set CPU limits in production? Why or why not?"]
  },
  38: {
    a: `When a pod is deleted, it's marked Terminating and removed from Service endpoints, while the kubelet runs the preStop hook if there is one, then sends SIGTERM to PID 1 of each container. The pod gets terminationGracePeriodSeconds, thirty by default, to exit, and after that the kubelet sends SIGKILL. Graceful shutdowns fail for a few common reasons. The app ignores SIGTERM, often because it isn't PID 1 and a shell wrapper swallows the signal. Or there's a race: endpoint removal and SIGTERM happen in parallel, so traffic may still arrive at a pod that's shutting down. The fixes are a short preStop sleep of a few seconds, an app that stops accepting new connections and drains in-flight requests on SIGTERM, using exec in the entrypoint or a small init like tini, and a grace period longer than your longest request. I'd pair this with a PodDisruptionBudget so voluntary disruptions don't take too many replicas down at once.`,
    f: ["Why does a preStop sleep help?","What happens to long-lived connections like websockets during a rollout?"]
  },
  39: {
    a: `I pick the controller by the workload's characteristics. A Deployment is for stateless, interchangeable replicas: web services and APIs, with rolling updates and easy scaling. A StatefulSet is for workloads that need stable identity: each pod gets a predictable name and DNS entry, its own persistent volume through volumeClaimTemplates, and ordered startup and shutdown. That fits databases, Kafka, and ZooKeeper. A DaemonSet runs one pod per node, or per selected node, which suits node-level agents like log collectors, monitoring exporters, CNI plugins, and storage drivers. A Job runs a task to completion, with retries, and a CronJob schedules Jobs on a cron expression, for batch processing, backups, and reports. A few practical notes: for CronJobs I set the concurrency policy and deadlines so overlapping or missed runs behave sensibly, and I don't put a database in a StatefulSet casually. Running stateful systems well takes operators, backups, and failover handling, so often a managed database is the better choice.`,
    f: ["When is a StatefulSet the wrong answer for a database?","What does a CronJob concurrencyPolicy control?"]
  },
  40: {
    a: `Liveness asks: is this container stuck beyond recovery? If it fails, the kubelet restarts the container. Readiness asks: can this pod take traffic right now? If it fails, the pod is removed from Service endpoints but not restarted. Startup asks: has the app finished booting? It holds off the other two until it passes, which protects slow starters. The danger of a bad liveness probe is that it turns a partial problem into an outage. If the probe checks a downstream dependency like the database, a database blip makes every pod fail liveness, and Kubernetes restarts the whole fleet at once, a self-inflicted cascading failure. Aggressive timeouts and thresholds do the same under load: a busy pod misses a probe, gets killed, and dumps its traffic on the others. So I keep liveness cheap and local, just asking whether the process is responsive. Dependency checks belong in readiness, if anywhere. I use a startup probe for slow apps and tune periods, timeouts, and thresholds from real behavior under load.`,
    f: ["Should readiness check the database? What are the trade-offs?","What happens if there is no readiness probe at all?"]
  },
  41: {
    a: `The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler changes the number of replicas, while the Vertical Pod Autoscaler changes each pod's resource requests and limits. The HPA formula is: desired replicas equals the ceiling of current replicas times current metric divided by target metric. For example, four pods at eighty percent CPU with a fifty percent target gives ceiling of six point four, so seven replicas. It reads metrics from metrics-server for CPU and memory, or from adapters like Prometheus Adapter or KEDA for custom and external metrics, and uses stabilization windows to avoid flapping. Utilization is relative to requests, so requests must be set. The conflict arises when both HPA and VPA act on the same CPU or memory metric: VPA changes requests, which changes the utilization percentage the HPA sees, and they oscillate. Safe combinations are HPA on CPU with VPA on memory, or running VPA in recommendation-only mode and applying its suggestions deliberately, or scaling horizontally on custom metrics like queue depth while VPA handles sizing.`,
    f: ["Why does VPA usually restart pods to apply changes?","When would you scale on queue depth rather than CPU?"]
  },
  42: {
    a: `These are the scheduling controls, and they work from different directions. A taint sits on a node and repels pods unless the pod has a matching toleration. A typical use is a GPU node pool: I taint the nodes dedicated equals gpu with NoSchedule, and only GPU workloads that tolerate it land there. The effect matters: NoSchedule blocks new pods, PreferNoSchedule is a soft hint, and NoExecute also evicts running pods. Note a toleration only permits scheduling; it doesn't attract, so GPU pods also need node affinity or a selector to choose those nodes. Node affinity is how a pod selects nodes by label, with required or preferred rules. Pod affinity places pods near other pods, for example a web tier close to its cache. Pod anti-affinity does the opposite, spreading replicas of the same app across different nodes or zones using a topologyKey like hostname, which is how I avoid all replicas sharing a failure domain. For even spreading at scale, I prefer topology spread constraints.`,
    f: ["Does a toleration guarantee a pod lands on the tainted node?","What is the difference between required and preferred affinity?"]
  },
  43: {
    a: `Topology spread constraints tell the scheduler to distribute matching pods evenly across failure domains such as zones or nodes, with a tunable maxSkew, which is the allowed difference in pod count between the most and least loaded domain. I specify the topologyKey, for example the zone label, a label selector for the pods, and whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule for a hard rule or ScheduleAnyway for a soft one. They're preferred over pod anti-affinity for several reasons. Anti-affinity is binary: a pod either can or can't be co-located, so with more replicas than domains, required anti-affinity leaves pods Pending, whereas spread constraints keep things balanced and still schedule. Anti-affinity is also more expensive to evaluate, because each pod is checked against others, which hurts scheduler performance at scale. Spread constraints express the actual intent, which is an even distribution across availability zones for resilience. I commonly use both: a zone-level constraint with maxSkew one and a node-level one as a soft rule.`,
    f: ["What happens with maxSkew when a zone has no capacity?","How do spread constraints behave during a rolling update?"]
  },
  44: {
    a: `The kube-scheduler handles each pending pod in two phases: filtering and scoring. In filtering, it eliminates nodes that can't run the pod. It checks whether the node has enough allocatable CPU and memory for the pod's requests, whether node selectors and affinity match, whether the pod tolerates the node's taints, whether the node is cordoned, and whether the required volumes can bind there, along with port conflicts. If no node passes, the pod stays Pending and the event lists why each node was rejected. In scoring, the remaining nodes are ranked by plugins: balanced resource allocation, image locality so nodes that already have the image score higher, inter-pod affinity and topology spread, and a strategy like least-allocated, which spreads load, or most-allocated, which bin-packs for cost. The highest score wins, with ties broken randomly. Then the scheduler binds the pod to the node. Importantly, scheduling uses requests, not actual usage, so inaccurate requests lead to poor placement. If needed, preemption can evict lower priority pods.`,
    f: ["Why does the scheduler look at requests rather than real usage?","How do you make the scheduler bin-pack instead of spread?"]
  },
  45: {
    a: `A PodDisruptionBudget limits how many pods of an application can be unavailable during voluntary disruptions. You define either minAvailable or maxUnavailable, as a number or percentage, plus a selector. It applies to actions that go through the Eviction API: kubectl drain, node pool upgrades, and cluster autoscaler scale-down. It does not cover involuntary events such as a node crash, hardware failure, or an OOM kill. During a drain, if evicting a pod would violate the budget, the eviction is refused and drain retries until the budget allows it, which keeps the service above its minimum. The classic failure is a budget that can never be satisfied: one replica with minAvailable one, or minAvailable equal to replicas, which blocks drains and upgrades indefinitely. So I align the budget with the replica count, for instance maxUnavailable one for most services, and make sure readiness probes are accurate, since the budget counts ready pods. For upgrades I monitor for blocked evictions and alert on PDBs with zero allowed disruptions.`,
    f: ["How do you diagnose a node upgrade stuck on a PDB?","Do PDBs apply to deleting a pod directly?"]
  },
  46: {
    a: `ConfigMaps hold non-sensitive configuration, and Secrets hold sensitive values; both can be injected as environment variables or mounted as files. Secrets are only base64-encoded by default, so encryption at rest and tight RBAC matter. The important operational difference is how updates propagate. Environment variables are read once when the container starts, so changing the ConfigMap does nothing until the pod restarts. Mounted files are eventually updated in place by the kubelet, but the application has to re-read them, and subPath mounts never update. For safe rollouts without downtime, I make config changes trigger a normal rolling update: include a checksum of the config as an annotation in the pod template, which Helm can do with a sha256 function, or use a tool like Reloader, or run kubectl rollout restart. That way new pods start with the new config, readiness probes gate traffic, and old pods are removed gradually, and rollback is simple. Another approach is immutable ConfigMaps with versioned names, so each change is a new object and the Deployment references it explicitly.`,
    f: ["Why doesn't a subPath mounted ConfigMap update?","What are the advantages of immutable ConfigMaps?"]
  },
  47: {
    a: `Minimal images such as distroless or scratch have no shell, so kubectl exec can't open one. The modern answer is ephemeral debug containers. With kubectl debug I attach a temporary container, for example busybox or netshoot, to the running pod, and with the target flag it shares the process namespace of the application container, so I can see its processes, read its proc entries, and use the pod's network namespace to test connections, run tcpdump, or curl localhost. That doesn't restart or change the original container. If I need to experiment more deeply, kubectl debug can copy the pod into a new debug pod with a shell, optionally with the image swapped. For node-level issues, kubectl debug node starts a privileged pod with the host filesystem mounted. Alongside that, there are the basics: logs with the previous flag for crashed instances, port-forward to hit the app directly, kubectl cp to extract files, and crictl or nsenter on the node. Ephemeral containers need to be permitted by RBAC, which is also a security consideration.`,
    f: ["Why are ephemeral containers restricted by RBAC?","How would you capture network traffic from a pod with no tcpdump?"]
  },
  48: {
    a: `Two parameters control a rolling update. maxSurge is how many extra pods above the desired count may exist during the rollout, and maxUnavailable is how many pods may be missing. Setting maxUnavailable to zero with maxSurge of one or a small percentage gives the safest, zero-downtime behaviour: a new pod must become Ready before an old one is removed. A larger surge makes the rollout faster but uses more capacity. This depends on correct readiness probes, otherwise Kubernetes considers a pod ready too early, and on graceful shutdown. For a canary, without extra tooling you can run a second small Deployment with the new image behind the same Service selector. With nine stable pods and one canary, roughly ten percent of traffic reaches the new version, based on pod counts. You watch error rates and latency and then promote. That's crude, because the split depends on replica ratios. With Argo Rollouts or Flagger you get explicit traffic weights, pauses, and automated analysis against Prometheus metrics, with automatic rollback if the canary degrades.`,
    f: ["Why does a rolling update need accurate readiness probes?","How does Argo Rollouts decide to roll back automatically?"]
  },
  49: {
    a: `Pod priority lets me tell the scheduler which pods matter more, using PriorityClass objects with a numeric value. If a high-priority pod can't be scheduled because the cluster is full, the scheduler can preempt: it finds a node where evicting lower-priority pods would make room, evicts the minimum necessary, respecting PodDisruptionBudgets where it can, and schedules the important pod. That's useful for protecting critical system components and revenue-critical services over batch jobs. The risk is cascading effects. If many high-priority pods appear at once, they can evict a wave of lower-priority pods, which then go Pending, possibly triggering autoscaler scale-ups, and if they in turn have other priorities they can displace further pods, causing churn. Misuse is the usual cause: if everything is marked high priority, priority loses meaning and preemption thrashes. My approach is a small number of well-defined classes, reserving the highest for system-critical pods, setting a low default for batch workloads, using preemptionPolicy Never where I want ordering but not eviction, and keeping enough headroom.`,
    f: ["What is preemptionPolicy Never used for?","How would you give batch jobs lower priority than services?"]
  },
  50: {
    a: `The container memory limit is enforced by the cgroup, and it counts far more than the application heap. So when a pod is OOMKilled while the heap looks fine, I look for the other memory consumers. Page cache from heavy file I/O counts toward the cgroup, although it is reclaimable, so it usually matters less. Off-heap and native memory is a big one: in the JVM that means direct buffers, metaspace, JNI libraries, and memory-mapped files. Thread stacks add up when an application creates many threads. Volumes of type emptyDir with medium Memory, and tmpfs mounts, count against the container's limit too. Also, older runtimes that aren't container-aware size their heap from the host's memory rather than the limit. To diagnose, kubectl describe confirms OOMKilled and exit code 137, kubectl top with containers shows live usage, and on the node I read the cgroup memory statistics to see rss, cache, and mapped file. Then I fix it by setting the heap to a fraction of the limit, leaving headroom, and raising the limit based on measured peak.`,
    f: ["How would you size a JVM heap inside a container limit?","Does page cache count toward the OOM kill decision?"]
  },
  51: {
    a: `The Cluster Autoscaler adds nodes when pods are Pending because no node has room, and removes nodes that are underused and whose pods can move elsewhere. It works with predefined node groups, such as AWS Auto Scaling groups or GCP managed instance groups, so it scales by increasing the size of a group of identical machines. Scale-down respects PodDisruptionBudgets, local storage, and annotations. Karpenter takes a different approach. It has no node groups: it watches pending pods, calculates what they need in aggregate, and launches the right instance types directly, choosing from a wide range, including spot. That makes it faster, since nodes come up in seconds without waiting on group abstractions, and better at bin-packing and cost optimisation. It also does consolidation, actively repacking workloads onto fewer or cheaper nodes, and handles node expiry for patching. The trade-offs are that Karpenter is most mature on AWS, and you need to define constraints in NodePools carefully. For both, accurate pod requests are essential.`,
    f: ["Why might the autoscaler not scale up even though pods are Pending?","How does Karpenter consolidation save cost?"]
  },
  52: {
    a: `A Service is a stable virtual IP and DNS name in front of a changing set of pods, selected by a label selector. The link between them is the EndpointSlice, which lists the IPs of matching pods that are Ready. Only ready pods are included, which is why readiness probes matter: a pod that fails readiness is removed from the Service's endpoints, though the Service itself remains. If no pods are ready, the endpoint list is empty and connections fail with refused or time out. So when debugging a Service I check the selector matches the labels, that targetPort matches the container port, and that the EndpointSlice has addresses. A headless Service sets clusterIP to None, so there's no virtual IP and no kube-proxy load balancing. DNS returns the individual pod IPs directly, as multiple A records. That's used by StatefulSets, where each pod needs a stable DNS name, and by clients that do their own load balancing or need to reach specific pods, such as databases and gRPC clients.`,
    f: ["Why would a Service exist but have no endpoints?","How do StatefulSet pods get individual DNS names?"]
  },
  53: {
    a: `Take pod A on node one talking to pod B on node two. Pod A sends to pod B's IP through its eth0, which is one end of a virtual ethernet pair. The other end lives in node one's root network namespace, attached to a bridge or handled directly by the CNI datapath. Node one then needs to know that B's IP belongs to node two's pod CIDR, which it learns through routes or the CNI's control plane. Now there are two common designs. With an overlay, such as Flannel VXLAN or Calico IP-in-IP, the packet is encapsulated inside another packet addressed to node two's IP, sent across the node network, and decapsulated on arrival. With routed or native networking, as in Calico BGP, or cloud VPC-native CNIs like the AWS and GKE ones, pod IPs are routable on the network, so there's no encapsulation, with better performance and easier debugging. On node two the packet is routed to B's veth and into its namespace. If the destination is a Service IP, kube-proxy or eBPF rewrites it to a pod IP first.`,
    f: ["What are the trade-offs between overlay and routed CNIs?","Where does a Service IP get translated to a pod IP?"]
  },
  54: {
    a: `kube-proxy makes Service virtual IPs work by programming each node's datapath so traffic to a Service IP is load-balanced to one of its backend pods. In iptables mode, it installs DNAT rules that pick a backend randomly. It's simple and reliable, but rules are evaluated linearly, so with thousands of Services the chains get huge and updates slow down. IPVS mode uses the kernel's IPVS with hash tables, giving constant-time lookups and proper balancing algorithms like round robin and least connections, which scales much better. eBPF, as in Cilium's kube-proxy replacement, goes further: it does Service load balancing in eBPF programs, often at the socket level, so there's no per-packet NAT chain, with better performance, observability, and consistent policy enforcement. My rule of thumb: iptables is fine for small to medium clusters, IPVS for large Service counts, and eBPF when I'm already running Cilium or want the best performance and visibility. Whichever mode, I watch for conntrack table limits, which cause drops under heavy load.`,
    f: ["What problems occur when the conntrack table fills up?","Why does iptables mode degrade with many Services?"]
  },
  55: {
    a: `CoreDNS resolves Service and pod names in the cluster. A Service is reachable at its name, namespace, svc, cluster.local. Each pod's resolv.conf lists search domains for its namespace and the cluster, the CoreDNS Service IP as nameserver, and ndots set to five. That ndots setting is a well-known trap. Any name with fewer than five dots is tried against each search domain first, so a lookup for api.example.com generates several failing queries before the real one succeeds. High-traffic applications generate a lot of extra DNS load and latency. Common failure modes: CoreDNS pods overloaded or crashing, a bad Corefile, upstream resolvers failing for external names, a NetworkPolicy blocking port 53 to CoreDNS, and the conntrack race on UDP that causes five-second delays. My fixes are: use fully qualified names with a trailing dot for external hosts, lower ndots in the pod dnsConfig, scale CoreDNS, and deploy NodeLocal DNSCache to cache close to the pod and avoid the race. I monitor CoreDNS latency and error metrics.`,
    f: ["Why does a trailing dot help a DNS lookup?","What does NodeLocal DNSCache change?"]
  },
  56: {
    a: `These expose workloads at different layers. A Service of type LoadBalancer works at layer four and provisions one cloud load balancer per Service. It's simple and works for any TCP or UDP protocol, but it costs one load balancer per Service and has no HTTP routing. Ingress works at layer seven: a shared controller, such as NGINX, Traefik, or a cloud load balancer, routes by host and path, terminates TLS, and lets many Services share one entry point. Its limitation is a minimal spec, so vendors add features through annotations, which makes configurations non-portable. Gateway API is the successor. It separates roles: GatewayClass for the infrastructure provider, Gateway for the cluster operator, and routes like HTTPRoute for application teams. It supports header and method matching, traffic weights, cross-namespace routing, and more protocols, in a portable, typed way. My guidance: use LoadBalancer for non-HTTP traffic or a single service, Ingress for existing HTTP setups, and Gateway API for new designs that need richer routing and clean team boundaries.`,
    f: ["How does Gateway API support multi-team ownership?","When is an Ingress annotation a portability risk?"]
  },
  57: {
    a: `By default Kubernetes networking is flat: any pod can talk to any other pod, in any namespace. NetworkPolicies add firewall rules for pod traffic, but only when the CNI enforces them, as Calico and Cilium do and plain Flannel doesn't. The key semantic is that a pod starts open, but as soon as any policy selects it for ingress or egress, that direction becomes deny by default, and only the rules listed are allowed; rules are additive. To implement zero trust, I start each namespace with a default-deny policy that selects all pods, with an empty podSelector and no rules. Then I add explicit allows per application: frontend to API on its port, API to database, and so on, using pod and namespace selectors. Egress needs care: I must explicitly allow DNS to CoreDNS and any required external destinations. I roll out in stages, using tools with flow visibility such as Cilium Hubble to see what traffic exists before enforcing, and I test in staging. NetworkPolicies are layer three and four, so identity-based mTLS in a mesh complements them.`,
    f: ["Why does default-deny egress often break DNS?","How would you roll out default deny safely on a live namespace?"]
  },
  58: {
    a: `Intermittent delays of almost exactly five seconds are the signature of the DNS conntrack race. On some older kernels, a client sends the A and AAAA queries in parallel over UDP from the same socket, and a race in the kernel's connection tracking and NAT insertion causes one packet to be dropped. The resolver then waits for its default five-second timeout before retrying, so the request succeeds but slowly. The ndots five setting makes it more likely because it multiplies the number of lookups. I confirm by testing DNS from the affected pods and seeing occasional five-second lookups, checking conntrack insert-failed counters, and capturing packets. Fixes: deploy NodeLocal DNSCache so pods query a local cache and avoid the race; add resolv.conf options such as single-request-reopen, or use TCP for DNS; reduce ndots or use fully qualified names for external hosts; and upgrade to a kernel where the race is fixed. It's also worth checking CoreDNS capacity and upstream health, since overload produces similar timeouts, but the fixed five-second value is the giveaway.`,
    f: ["How do you prove it's the conntrack race and not CoreDNS overload?","What does single-request-reopen do?"]
  },
  59: {
    a: `Kubernetes separates what an application asks for from how storage is provided. A StorageClass describes a type of storage and the provisioner behind it, for example gp3 or SSD. A PersistentVolumeClaim is a pod's request: a size, an access mode, and a class. A PersistentVolume is the actual provisioned disk, bound one to one to a claim. With dynamic provisioning, the flow is: a pod references a PVC; the PVC names a StorageClass; the CSI provisioner for that class creates a real disk in the cloud; a PV object is created and bound to the claim; and the volume is attached and mounted into the pod. Two details matter in practice. Access modes: ReadWriteOnce means one node at a time, which is what block disks give you, while ReadWriteMany needs shared file storage like NFS or EFS. And volumeBindingMode WaitForFirstConsumer delays creation until the pod is scheduled, so the disk lands in the right zone. The reclaim policy decides whether the disk is deleted or retained when the claim goes.`,
    f: ["What does WaitForFirstConsumer solve?","Delete versus Retain reclaim policy: which would you use for production data?"]
  },
  60: {
    a: `CSI, the Container Storage Interface, standardises how Kubernetes talks to storage systems through out-of-tree drivers, so vendors ship their own driver without changing Kubernetes. A driver has two parts. The controller plugin, usually a Deployment with sidecars such as external-provisioner and external-attacher, talks to the cloud or storage API to create, delete, attach, and detach volumes. The node plugin, a DaemonSet, runs on every node and does the local work, registered through the node-driver-registrar. When a pod using a volume is scheduled, the sequence is: first, ControllerPublishVolume attaches the disk to the chosen node through the cloud API. Then NodeStageVolume formats the disk if needed and mounts it at a global staging path on the node. Then NodePublishVolume bind-mounts that path into the pod's container directory. Unmounting reverses it. Understanding the split helps debugging: attach failures show up in VolumeAttachment objects and controller logs, while mount and permission failures show up in the node plugin and kubelet logs.`,
    f: ["Where would you look if a volume attaches but the pod can't mount it?","What do the CSI sidecar containers do?"]
  },
  61: {
    a: `This is stickiness by design. A StatefulSet pod is bound to its own PVC, and for block storage that PVC is a zonal, ReadWriteOnce disk, which can be attached to only one node at a time and only in its own availability zone. If the old node died uncleanly, the cloud or the VolumeAttachment object may still show the disk attached there, so the new node can't attach it, and the pod stays in ContainerCreating with multi-attach or attach timeout errors. Another cause is zone mismatch: the pod is scheduled to a node in another zone from the disk. To resolve it, I look at the pod events and kubectl get volumeattachment to find the stale attachment. If the old node is truly gone, I delete the Node object, or force delete the pod if I'm certain the node is dead, then the attach controller detaches the volume and the pod can start. I'm careful because forcing this while the old node is still writing can corrupt data. Prevention: use topology-aware scheduling, replicate data at the application layer, and make sure nodes drain cleanly.`,
    f: ["Why is force-deleting a StatefulSet pod risky?","How does application-level replication reduce this problem?"]
  },
  62: {
    a: `CSI covers all three. For expansion, the StorageClass needs allowVolumeExpansion set to true, then I patch the PVC to request a larger size. The CSI driver grows the underlying disk, and on modern drivers the filesystem is resized online, often without restarting the pod; some need a restart. Expansion is one way only, you can't shrink. For snapshots, I create a VolumeSnapshot referencing the PVC and a VolumeSnapshotClass, and the driver takes a point-in-time snapshot of the disk. To restore, I create a new PVC whose dataSource points at the snapshot. But a storage snapshot is crash-consistent, not application-consistent, so for databases I either quiesce or flush before the snapshot, or use application-aware backups such as logical dumps or the database operator's backup feature. For real backup and disaster recovery, I use a tool like Velero, which captures Kubernetes objects and volume snapshots and stores them off-cluster in object storage, ideally in another region. And I test restores regularly, because that is the only proof the backup works.`,
    f: ["Why isn't a volume snapshot enough for a database backup?","What does Velero back up that a snapshot doesn't?"]
  },
  63: {
    a: `A service mesh such as Istio or Linkerd injects a sidecar proxy into each pod, or uses node-level proxies in newer ambient designs, and uses it to secure and manage service-to-service traffic transparently. For mutual TLS, the control plane acts as a certificate authority and issues short-lived certificates to each workload, tied to a SPIFFE identity based on its service account. Traffic is redirected through the proxy using iptables or eBPF. When service A calls B, proxy A presents its certificate, proxy B verifies it and presents its own, and the traffic is encrypted, with authentication in both directions. The application code doesn't change. The mesh also provides retries, timeouts, circuit breaking, traffic splitting for canaries, and uniform L7 metrics and tracing. The costs are real: extra CPU and memory per pod, added latency per hop, more moving parts to operate and upgrade, a steep learning curve, and harder debugging because traffic passes through proxies. So I adopt a mesh when I need its capabilities, like compliance-driven mTLS across many services, not by default.`,
    f: ["When would you not adopt a service mesh?","What is the ambient mesh model trying to fix?"]
  },
  64: {
    a: `At scale, IP address planning becomes a hard constraint because pod and service ranges are difficult to change later, often requiring a cluster rebuild. Each node is given a slice of the cluster's pod CIDR; a slash twenty-four per node gives about two hundred fifty pods per node, and the overall CIDR limits the number of nodes times pods per node. On VPC-native clusters, such as GKE alias IPs or the AWS VPC CNI, pods use real VPC addresses, so a large cluster can exhaust the VPC or subnet itself. Mitigations: size the ranges generously at creation for peak nodes and density; reduce the maximum pods per node if addresses are tight; use secondary ranges or non-routable pod ranges with masquerading; use prefix delegation on AWS; and plan the service CIDR separately. Dual-stack, supporting IPv4 and IPv6 together, is another way to escape IPv4 limits, but it adds operational complexity across load balancers, policies, and tooling, so it needs testing. The practical lesson: do IP capacity planning early and document it.`,
    f: ["How does the AWS VPC CNI use IP addresses differently from an overlay?","Can you change the pod CIDR on a running cluster?"]
  },
  65: {
    a: `Each volume type fits a different need. emptyDir is scratch space tied to the pod's life: it survives container restarts but is deleted with the pod. It's fine for caches and temporary files, and with medium Memory it's a RAM-backed tmpfs that counts against the container's memory limit. hostPath mounts a directory from the node. It's tied to that node, behaves differently across nodes, and is a security risk because it can expose the host filesystem, so I limit it to node agents. A local PersistentVolume uses a node's local disk through the PV mechanism, with node affinity so scheduling is correct. It gives the best performance, such as NVMe for databases and caches, but the data is lost if the node is lost, and the pod is pinned to that node, so it needs application-level replication. Network storage, such as EBS, Persistent Disk, NFS, or Ceph, is durable and survives node loss, and can be reattached elsewhere, but has network latency and, for block, zonal attachment limits. My default for important data is network-backed PVCs.`,
    f: ["When is a local PV worth the operational cost?","Why does emptyDir with medium Memory risk OOM kills?"]
  },
  66: {
    a: `gRPC uses long-lived HTTP/2 connections that multiplex many requests, and that interacts badly with connection-level load balancing. A Kubernetes Service, or a layer four cloud load balancer, balances connections, not requests. So a gRPC client opens one connection, it is pinned to a single backend pod, and all its requests go there. Load becomes uneven, and when you scale up, new pods get no traffic until clients reconnect. There are three fixes. First, use a layer seven proxy that understands HTTP/2 and balances per request: an Envoy-based ingress or gateway, or a service mesh like Linkerd or Istio, which does it transparently. Second, client-side load balancing: use a headless Service so DNS returns all pod IPs, and have the gRPC client use a round-robin policy and re-resolve DNS periodically. Third, limit connection lifetime with settings like max connection age on the server so clients reconnect and rebalance, with graceful draining. I also make sure the ingress or cloud load balancer is configured for HTTP/2 and has suitable idle timeouts, and that health checks use gRPC health checking.`,
    f: ["Why does scaling up not help a gRPC service behind a ClusterIP Service?","What does max connection age do?"]
  },
  67: {
    a: `An Operator packages human operational knowledge for an application into a controller. You define a CustomResourceDefinition, which adds a new resource type, for example a PostgresCluster. A user creates an instance of it, stating the desired outcome: three nodes, version sixteen, daily backups. The operator watches those custom resources and continuously reconciles reality toward that spec, creating the StatefulSets, Services, and Secrets, but also doing the domain-specific work. That's why operators suit stateful applications: a StatefulSet gives stable identity and storage but knows nothing about the application. An operator adds primary election and failover, replica configuration, backups and point-in-time recovery, rolling upgrades in the right order, scaling, and certificate handling. Examples are CloudNativePG for Postgres and Strimzi for Kafka. The pattern is the same control loop that Kubernetes itself uses. When choosing one, I check maturity, how it handles failure and upgrades, and its backup story, since an immature operator on production data is a risk. Building my own is possible with Kubebuilder or Operator SDK, but I would avoid it unless necessary.`,
    f: ["What is a reconcile loop and why must it be idempotent?","How do you evaluate whether an operator is production ready?"]
  },
  68: {
    a: `With a Service of type LoadBalancer, traffic goes from the internet to the cloud load balancer, which targets a NodePort on the cluster's nodes, then kube-proxy on that node, and finally to a pod. The setting externalTrafficPolicy controls the behaviour. With Cluster, the default, the load balancer can send traffic to any node, and kube-proxy may forward it to a pod on a different node. That adds a hop and, because it SNATs the packet, hides the real client IP from the application, but load is spread evenly. With Local, only nodes that actually run a backend pod accept the traffic. There's no second hop, and the client source IP is preserved, which matters for logging, allow-listing, and rate limiting. The downside is that load can be uneven if pods are spread unevenly across nodes, and the load balancer's health checks must correctly exclude nodes without local pods. During rolling updates, connection draining matters too. Some clouds can bypass NodePort entirely by sending traffic directly to pod IPs, which improves latency and visibility.`,
    f: ["How do you preserve the client IP behind a cloud load balancer?","What problem does Local policy create for pod distribution?"]
  },
  69: {
    a: `Running a database on Kubernetes is viable, but the risks centre on data safety during failures, so I'd be deliberate. The real risks are: when a node fails, the volume may take minutes to detach and reattach, causing downtime; misuse of force detach on a ReadWriteOnce volume can lead to split-brain and corruption; a pod could be rescheduled away from its zonal disk; an aggressive liveness probe can restart a healthy but busy database; and noisy neighbours or control plane problems cause churn. Mitigations: use a mature operator such as CloudNativePG or Strimzi for failover, backups, and upgrades rather than a hand-rolled StatefulSet; run at least three replicas with application-level replication so losing a node doesn't lose data; spread them across zones; use anti-affinity and PDBs; give the database dedicated nodes via taints, with Guaranteed QoS and fast storage; use conservative probes; and, most importantly, keep tested backups with point-in-time recovery stored off-cluster. And be honest about the alternative: a managed database often moves these risks to the provider, which is frequently the better choice.`,
    f: ["When would you recommend a managed database instead?","Why are liveness probes dangerous on databases?"]
  },
  70: {
    a: `RBAC answers who can do what to which resources, using four objects. A Role grants permissions inside one namespace: verbs on resources. A ClusterRole is cluster-wide permissions, and also covers cluster-scoped resources like nodes and non-resource URLs. A RoleBinding grants a Role or a ClusterRole to subjects, which are users, groups, or service accounts, within one namespace. A ClusterRoleBinding grants a ClusterRole across the entire cluster. The subtle combination that confuses people is that a RoleBinding can reference a ClusterRole. The permissions come from the ClusterRole, but they apply only inside the binding's namespace. That lets me define a permission set once, like view or edit, and reuse it per namespace. RBAC is additive: there are no deny rules, so the union of all bindings applies, and I audit with kubectl auth can-i. Good practice is least privilege, namespace-scoped bindings, groups instead of individual users, avoiding wildcards, and never binding cluster-admin to applications. Also, some permissions, such as creating pods or reading secrets, are effectively powerful and need extra care.`,
    f: ["Why is there no deny rule in RBAC?","Which verbs or resources are effectively privilege escalation?"]
  },
  71: {
    a: `PodSecurityPolicy was removed in Kubernetes one point twenty-five. Its replacement is Pod Security Admission, a built-in admission controller driven by labels on namespaces, which enforces the Pod Security Standards. There are three standards. Privileged is unrestricted. Baseline blocks known privilege escalations: no privileged containers, no host namespaces, restricted host paths and capabilities. Restricted follows current hardening best practice: must run as non-root, drop all capabilities, use a seccomp profile, and disallow privilege escalation. There are three modes, set per namespace: enforce rejects violating pods, audit records violations in the audit log, and warn shows a warning to the user. A good rollout is to start with warn and audit on restricted to see what would break, fix the workloads, then switch on enforce. PSA is deliberately simple. It can't do exceptions per workload or custom rules, so for finer control, or mutation, I pair it with Kyverno or Gatekeeper, or ValidatingAdmissionPolicy. I'd apply restricted to application namespaces and baseline or privileged only to system namespaces that need it.`,
    f: ["How do you migrate from PSP to PSA without breaking workloads?","What can't PSA do that Kyverno can?"]
  },
  72: {
    a: `A hardened securityContext reduces the damage if a container is compromised. The core settings are: runAsNonRoot true with an explicit non-zero user, so the process can't run as UID zero; allowPrivilegeEscalation false, which blocks gaining more privileges than the parent, such as via setuid binaries; readOnlyRootFilesystem true, so an attacker can't modify the container's files, with writable emptyDir mounts only where needed, such as a temp directory; capabilities dropped entirely, adding back only specific ones, like binding a low port if truly required; and a seccomp profile of RuntimeDefault, which blocks dozens of rarely needed, dangerous system calls. I'd also avoid privileged mode, host namespaces, and hostPath mounts, set an fsGroup when volumes need group access, and disable service account token mounting if the API isn't needed. The image must support this, so it has a non-root user and doesn't write to unexpected places. I then enforce it with Pod Security Admission at the restricted level, and validate with kube-bench and runtime tools. For the CKS, I'd know each field and what it prevents.`,
    f: ["What typically breaks when you enable readOnlyRootFilesystem?","Which capability would you add back to bind port 80 as non-root?"]
  },
  73: {
    a: `A container is just a process with namespaces and cgroups, sharing the host kernel, so an escape exploits a misconfiguration or kernel bug to reach the host. The common vectors are: privileged containers, which have nearly full host access; mounting the container runtime socket, which lets you control the runtime and start privileged containers; hostPath mounts of sensitive directories; powerful capabilities like SYS_ADMIN; sharing the host PID or network namespace; and kernel vulnerabilities, since the kernel is shared. The defence is layered. Prevent: don't grant privilege, drop capabilities, avoid host mounts and namespaces, run as non-root with a read-only filesystem, and enforce this by Pod Security Admission and policy engines. Reduce kernel exposure: seccomp, AppArmor or SELinux, and sandboxed runtimes such as gVisor or Kata Containers for untrusted workloads. Limit what an escape yields: separate node pools for sensitive workloads, minimal service account permissions, network policies, and no cloud credentials on nodes. Detect: Falco for suspicious runtime behaviour. And keep the kernel and runtime patched.`,
    f: ["Why is mounting the Docker or containerd socket so dangerous?","When do gVisor or Kata Containers justify their overhead?"]
  },
  74: {
    a: `Workload Identity on GKE, and IRSA on EKS, let a pod assume a cloud identity without any stored credentials. You map a Kubernetes ServiceAccount to a cloud IAM identity, a Google service account or an AWS role, using an annotation and a trust relationship. The pod receives a projected, audience-scoped service account token, an OIDC JWT, and the cloud's security token service exchanges it for short-lived cloud credentials, scoped to that role and rotated automatically. This is better than the alternatives for three reasons. Node service accounts mean every pod on the node inherits the node's cloud permissions, so a compromised pod gets broad access. Static keys stored in Secrets or environment variables are long-lived, can leak, and need manual rotation. With workload identity, permissions are per workload and least-privilege, credentials are short-lived with nothing to leak or rotate, and the audit logs show which workload did what. Operationally, I give each workload its own service account, restrict the node's own permissions to the minimum, and block pod access to the node metadata server.`,
    f: ["How do you stop pods reaching the node metadata endpoint?","What is the trust relationship in IRSA?"]
  },
  75: {
    a: `Admission control is the stage where the API server intercepts a request after authentication and authorisation, but before it is saved to etcd. It can mutate objects and validate them, which makes it the enforcement point for policy. There are two webhook types. Mutating webhooks can modify the object, for example injecting a sidecar, adding labels, or setting defaults. Validating webhooks run afterwards and can only accept or reject. For policy, OPA Gatekeeper uses the Rego language, with ConstraintTemplates and Constraints. It's very powerful for complex logic but has a steeper learning curve. Kyverno uses Kubernetes-native YAML policies and can validate, mutate, generate resources, and verify image signatures, so it is easier for most teams. There's also the built-in ValidatingAdmissionPolicy, which uses CEL expressions inside the API server, avoiding webhook latency and availability risk. Operationally, webhooks are a risk: a slow or unavailable webhook with failurePolicy Fail can block the cluster. So I set short timeouts, exclude system namespaces, run multiple replicas, and begin policies in audit mode before enforcing.`,
    f: ["How can a failing webhook break cluster operations?","When would you pick ValidatingAdmissionPolicy over Kyverno?"]
  },
  76: {
    a: `Supply-chain security is making sure that what runs in the cluster is exactly what you built from trusted sources, with known contents. It has four pillars. Scanning: tools like Trivy or Grype check images for known vulnerabilities in CI, failing the build on critical findings, and re-scan in the registry as new CVEs appear. SBOM: generate a software bill of materials with a tool like Syft, so I know every component inside an image and can find which images are affected when a vulnerability drops. Signing: sign images and attestations, such as build provenance, with cosign from Sigstore, ideally with keyless signing tied to the CI identity, aiming at SLSA levels. Admission: at the cluster, a policy engine such as Kyverno or the Sigstore policy controller rejects any image that is unsigned, comes from an unapproved registry, or lacks the required attestations. I also pin by digest, use minimal base images, and protect the build system itself, since the pipeline is part of the attack surface. Together this gives a verifiable chain from source to running container.`,
    f: ["What does keyless signing with cosign mean?","Why pin an image by digest rather than a tag?"]
  },
  77: {
    a: `Native Secrets are only base64-encoded in etcd and are easy to over-expose, so I improve this in layers. First, encrypt etcd at rest using an EncryptionConfiguration with a KMS provider, so etcd holds ciphertext. Second, tight RBAC on the secrets resource and audit logging of access. Third, keep the source of truth outside the cluster, in Vault or a cloud secret manager, and sync it with the External Secrets Operator, which creates Kubernetes Secrets automatically and supports rotation. Fourth, for the strongest model, the Secrets Store CSI driver mounts secrets directly into the pod at runtime, so nothing is persisted in etcd. Fifth, use dynamic secrets, such as Vault generating short-lived database credentials per workload that expire automatically, which limits the damage of a leak. For GitOps, never commit plain secrets: use Sealed Secrets or SOPS so only encrypted values are in Git, or reference external secrets. Also, prefer file mounts over environment variables, rotate regularly, and give workloads access through workload identity rather than long-lived tokens.`,
    f: ["What are the trade-offs between External Secrets Operator and the CSI driver?","How do dynamic secrets limit blast radius?"]
  },
  78: {
    a: `The API server is the front door of the cluster, so I harden it and the control plane in several areas. For access: strong authentication, using OIDC or cloud IAM, with anonymous auth disabled and no shared static credentials; RBAC kept to least privilege, with no casual cluster-admin; and the node authorizer and admission controllers enabled. Visibility: enable audit logging with a sensible policy and ship it to a central store, since without it there's no forensics. Network: keep the control plane private or limit it with authorised networks, and use TLS everywhere with certificate rotation. etcd holds all data including secrets, so I encrypt it at rest with a KMS provider, require mTLS between the API server and etcd, restrict network access to etcd, and take encrypted backups. On the nodes, I secure the kubelet by disabling anonymous access, setting authorisation mode to Webhook, and turning on rotation. I protect component config file permissions, and then verify against the CIS benchmark with kube-bench. On managed clusters, much of this is the provider's job, but access and audit are still mine.`,
    f: ["What would you put in an audit policy and where would the logs go?","How do you secure the kubelet API?"]
  },
  79: {
    a: `Build-time scanning cannot catch what happens after a container is running: an attacker exploiting an application vulnerability, a stolen credential, or a zero-day. Runtime threats include a reverse shell started in a container, unexpected processes such as a crypto miner or recon tools, reading sensitive files like etc shadow, writing to system directories, unexpected outbound connections to command-and-control servers, and privilege escalation or escape attempts. Falco watches system calls using eBPF or a kernel module and matches them against rules, for example shell spawned in a container, write below etc, or outbound connection to an unusual address, then raises alerts that I route to the SIEM or incident channel. Tetragon, from Cilium, also uses eBPF, but can go beyond detecting: it can enforce, for instance killing a process or blocking an action in the kernel, with low overhead and Kubernetes-aware context. The value is detection and fast response, such as isolating the pod with a network policy. The challenge is tuning rules to reduce noise, so I start with the default ruleset, review alerts, and tailor for my workloads.`,
    f: ["How do you reduce false positives from Falco?","What's the difference between detection and enforcement at runtime?"]
  },
  80: {
    a: `Securing a multi-tenant cluster means stacking isolation layers, and being honest that it's weaker than separate clusters. Per tenant I use a namespace as the basic boundary, with RBAC limiting the tenant's service accounts and users to their own namespaces. ResourceQuota caps CPU, memory, and object counts to stop noisy neighbours, and LimitRange sets default and maximum requests per pod. NetworkPolicies default to deny cross-namespace traffic. Pod Security Admission, or policy engines, enforce restricted security settings per namespace, and policies restrict what tenants may create, such as no hostPath, no privileged pods, only approved registries. For hostile or untrusted tenants, I add node-level isolation with dedicated node pools using taints and tolerations, sandboxed runtimes such as gVisor or Kata via RuntimeClass, and separate secrets management and identity. The weak points to acknowledge are the shared control plane and shared kernel: a noisy tenant can load the API server, and a kernel escape affects everyone. For strong guarantees, such as regulated or unrelated customers, I use separate clusters or virtual clusters.`,
    f: ["When is a namespace per tenant not enough?","How do virtual clusters compare to separate clusters?"]
  },
  81: {
    a: `Every pod runs as a ServiceAccount, and its token authenticates to the API server with that account's RBAC permissions. By default, the token is automatically mounted into every pod under var run secrets. If the pod doesn't need to call the Kubernetes API, which is true for most application pods, that mounted token is an unnecessary credential. An attacker who gains code execution in the pod can read it and use it against the API server, so it's an easy lateral movement path, especially if the service account is over-privileged. Mitigations: set automountServiceAccountToken to false on the service account or the pod when the API isn't needed; give workloads their own service accounts with minimal RBAC instead of default; and use bound, projected tokens. Since Kubernetes one point twenty-two, tokens are short-lived, audience-scoped, bound to the pod, and rotated automatically, unlike the old non-expiring token Secrets, so a stolen token is far less valuable. Projected tokens are also what powers workload identity to cloud providers, with a specific audience.`,
    f: ["What was wrong with the old long-lived service account token Secrets?","How does token projection enable workload identity?"]
  },
  82: {
    a: `I'd run it as a short incident process. First, triage and assess exposure. Is the vulnerability exploitable in our context: is the vulnerable component reachable, is there a known exploit, are the workloads internet-facing or privileged? I use SBOMs and registry scans, with Trivy across all images, to inventory exactly which images and running workloads are affected, and prioritise by severity and exposure. Second, contain if it's actively exploited: tighten NetworkPolicies, scale down or isolate exposed services, add an admission rule blocking the vulnerable image from new scheduling, and turn up runtime detection with Falco for signs of exploitation. Third, remediate: update the base image, rebuild and re-scan the affected images through CI, and roll them out by priority using the normal pipeline with canary or progressive delivery, which is much easier if base images and build templates are centralised. Fourth, verify: re-scan deployed workloads and confirm none still runs the vulnerable image. Finally, a short retrospective: how quickly did we find affected images, and how do we automate rebuilds and keep an up-to-date inventory?`,
    f: ["How do you decide whether a CVE is actually exploitable in your environment?","How do you make base image updates automatic?"]
  },
  83: {
    a: `Every API request goes through three gates in order. Authentication asks who you are: client certificates, OIDC tokens, service account tokens, or cloud IAM. Authorisation asks whether you are allowed to do this action: RBAC mostly, plus the node authoriser and webhooks. Admission asks whether this particular object should be accepted or changed: first mutating admission, then schema validation, then validating admission such as Pod Security Admission and policy engines, and only then is the object written to etcd. Knowing the order helps with diagnosis from the error message. Unauthorized, a 401, means authentication failed, like an expired token or certificate. Forbidden, a 403, saying a user cannot list pods means authorisation failed, so I check RBAC with auth can-i. An error saying a request was denied by a webhook or policy, or that a pod violates a security standard, is admission. It also tells me where to put controls: identity in authentication, who-can-do-what in RBAC, and content rules, like no privileged containers or required labels, in admission.`,
    f: ["Which HTTP status codes map to authentication and authorisation failures?","Why do mutating webhooks run before validating ones?"]
  },
  84: {
    a: `The CIS Kubernetes Benchmark is a widely used hardening checklist, and kube-bench automates checking a cluster against it. It inspects the control plane component flags for the API server, controller manager, scheduler, and etcd; file permissions and ownership for configuration files and certificates; kubelet settings such as anonymous auth being off, authorisation mode Webhook, and certificate rotation; etcd encryption and TLS; and whether audit logging is enabled. I run it as a job on the nodes, targeting control plane, node, etcd, and policies, and it outputs pass, fail, or warn for each control with remediation guidance. I then work through failures by risk, fix the configuration, and re-run, ideally in CI or on a schedule to catch drift. On managed services such as GKE, EKS, and AKS the provider secures the control plane, so I focus on the worker node checks and the policy items, and use provider-specific benchmark variants. kube-bench covers configuration, not workloads, so I pair it with image scanning, policy enforcement, and runtime detection.`,
    f: ["Which kube-bench results matter on a managed cluster?","How would you track CIS compliance over time?"]
  },
  85: {
    a: `These are Linux kernel security mechanisms that restrict what a container can do, which adds defence in depth below the Kubernetes layer. Seccomp filters system calls: a process can only invoke the syscalls the profile allows. The RuntimeDefault profile blocks dozens of rarely needed and dangerous syscalls and is a cheap, widely compatible win; custom profiles can be tighter. AppArmor is a path-based mandatory access control system: a profile restricts which files a process can access and which capabilities and network operations it can use. It's the default on Ubuntu and Debian. SELinux is label-based mandatory access control: policy is enforced using security labels on processes and files, the default on Red Hat family systems, and it's very strong but more complex. In Kubernetes I set seccompProfile in the securityContext, and AppArmor and SELinux through their respective fields and options. Which to use depends largely on the host operating system, since a node uses AppArmor or SELinux but not both. My baseline is RuntimeDefault seccomp everywhere, plus the OS-native MAC profile for sensitive workloads.`,
    f: ["Why is RuntimeDefault seccomp a good default?","Can a node run both AppArmor and SELinux?"]
  },
  86: {
    a: `I'd treat it as a security incident and follow detect, contain, preserve, eradicate, and recover. Detection signals are CPU pinned with no legitimate workload change, unexpected outbound connections to unknown IPs or mining pools, Falco alerts for unexpected processes or a shell in a container, and files or processes that aren't in the image. Containment first: apply a deny-all NetworkPolicy to that pod to cut command-and-control and exfiltration without destroying evidence. Preserve evidence before killing anything: capture logs, process lists, network connections, and a snapshot of the container filesystem or the node disk. If I suspect an escape, cordon the node and check other pods on it. Then investigate how it got in: a vulnerable application, an exposed dashboard, a leaked credential, or a poisoned image. Eradicate: delete and redeploy from a known-good image, rotate any secrets or tokens the pod could access, and review the service account's permissions and cloud credentials. Then fix the root cause, tighten policies, and add detection so it can't happen silently again, and write a postmortem.`,
    f: ["Why isolate with a network policy instead of deleting the pod immediately?","What credentials would you rotate after this?"]
  },
  87: {
    a: `I monitor in layers, because each has its own signals. For the control plane: API server request latency and error rate, etcd health and disk latency, and the scheduler and controller queues. For nodes: CPU, memory, disk and network usage, node conditions like pressure and Ready, and kubelet health. For Kubernetes workloads: pod restarts, OOMKills, Pending pods, readiness failures, and HPA behaviour. For applications: the golden signals of latency, traffic, errors, and saturation, or the RED method. The data sources are node-exporter for OS metrics, kube-state-metrics for object state such as desired versus ready replicas and PVC status, cAdvisor and the kubelet for container metrics, and the applications' own instrumentation, all scraped by Prometheus and shown in Grafana. Alerting matters more than dashboards: page on symptoms that affect users, like error rate and latency against SLOs, and send cause-level signals, such as restarts and pressure, to lower-urgency channels. I'd also watch the monitoring system itself and control cardinality so costs and query performance stay sane.`,
    f: ["Which alerts would page someone and which would only ticket?","What does kube-state-metrics provide that node-exporter doesn't?"]
  },
  88: {
    a: `Prometheus pulls metrics, and in Kubernetes it finds targets dynamically using service discovery. It watches the Kubernetes API through kubernetes_sd_configs, with roles such as pod, service, endpoints, node, and ingress. Relabeling rules then decide which discovered targets to scrape and rewrite their labels, for instance keeping only pods with an annotation, or attaching namespace and pod labels. Prometheus then scrapes the metrics path on each target at the configured interval, and as pods come and go the target list updates automatically. With the Prometheus Operator, you don't hand-write that configuration. A ServiceMonitor is a custom resource that selects Services by label and describes which port, path, and interval to scrape. A PodMonitor does the same directly for pods. The operator translates these into Prometheus configuration and reloads it, which lets each application team own the monitoring definition for its own service, alongside its deployment manifests. Common problems are a selector that doesn't match, a port name mismatch, or the Prometheus instance not selecting the ServiceMonitor's namespace or labels.`,
    f: ["Why might a ServiceMonitor exist but Prometheus show no target?","What does relabeling do in a scrape config?"]
  },
  89: {
    a: `Metrics, logs, and traces answer different questions, and I need all three to debug a distributed system. Metrics tell me what is happening and how much: aggregated numbers that are cheap to store and ideal for dashboards and alerting. Logs tell me what happened in detail: discrete events with context. Traces tell me where time is spent, following a single request across services. In Kubernetes, for metrics I run Prometheus scraping each service's metrics endpoint, Grafana for dashboards, and Alertmanager for notifications, usually through the kube-prometheus-stack. For logs, applications write to stdout, and a DaemonSet collector such as Fluent Bit or Promtail tails the container log files and ships them to Loki, Elasticsearch, or a cloud logging service. For traces, applications are instrumented with OpenTelemetry, which sends spans to a collector and then to Tempo or Jaeger. The real value comes from correlation: consistent labels, a trace ID in every log line, and exemplars linking metrics to traces, so I can go from an alert to the slow trace to the exact log lines.`,
    f: ["How do you correlate a metric spike with a trace and its logs?","What is OpenTelemetry's role versus a backend like Jaeger?"]
  },
  90: {
    a: `Containers are ephemeral: when a pod is deleted, rescheduled, or evicted, anything written to its local filesystem disappears. So logs must leave the pod immediately. The standard pattern is: the application writes to stdout and stderr; the container runtime writes that to log files on the node under var log containers; a DaemonSet collector such as Fluent Bit, Fluentd, or Promtail tails those files, enriches them with Kubernetes metadata like pod, namespace, and labels, and ships them to a central store such as Loki, Elasticsearch, or a cloud logging service, where I query them in Grafana or Kibana. Writing to files inside the pod is a bad idea for several reasons: the logs vanish with the pod, files consume the container's writable layer and can fill the node's disk, you need extra tooling to rotate them, and collectors can't find them in a uniform way. If an application insists on files, a sidecar can tail the file to stdout. I also use structured JSON logs, set retention and log levels deliberately, and watch the volume, since logging cost grows quickly.`,
    f: ["What happens to logs when a node dies?","How do you control logging cost at scale?"]
  },
  91: {
    a: `An SLO turns reliability into a measurable target. I pick an SLI, the signal that reflects user experience, for example the proportion of requests that succeed and complete under three hundred milliseconds. The SLO is the target, say ninety-nine point nine percent over thirty days, and the error budget is what's left, so zero point one percent of requests may fail. Alerting on a static threshold like error rate above one percent is either noisy or slow. Burn-rate alerting measures how fast the budget is being consumed. A burn rate of one means exactly using the budget over the window. I use multiple windows: a fast alert, for example a burn rate around fourteen over one hour with a short confirmation window, pages immediately for severe outages; a slower alert, like burn rate six over six hours, catches smaller sustained problems; and a slow burn becomes a ticket. Combining a long and a short window prevents paging after the problem is already over. In Kubernetes I compute the SLI from ingress or service mesh metrics in Prometheus, using recording rules, and tools like Sloth generate the rules.`,
    f: ["Why combine a long and a short window in burn-rate alerts?","How do you choose an SLI for a service?"]
  },
  92: {
    a: `The timeline is deliberately conservative to avoid flapping. The kubelet posts heartbeats through node leases. When they stop, after about forty seconds, the node controller marks the node NotReady or Unknown. The node also gets taints, not-ready and unreachable, with NoExecute. Pods tolerate those taints for three hundred seconds by default, the tolerationSeconds, so about five minutes later the pods are evicted. Then controllers such as the ReplicaSet notice the missing pods and create replacements on other nodes, which still need to be scheduled, pull images, and pass readiness. So realistically it takes five to six minutes before traffic recovers fully, though a Service stops routing to those pods earlier because their endpoints go not-ready. StatefulSets and ReadWriteOnce volumes add more delay, since the disk must be detached from the dead node first. It's slow on purpose: a brief network blip shouldn't trigger a mass reschedule. To speed it up, I can lower tolerationSeconds for latency-critical workloads, run enough replicas across zones to absorb the loss meanwhile, and use health checks and autoscaling.`,
    f: ["How would you shorten the failover time for a critical service?","Why does a Service stop routing before the pod is evicted?"]
  },
  93: {
    a: `I upgrade in a fixed order and by rules. Kubernetes supports upgrading one minor version at a time, never skipping, so one point twenty-eight to twenty-nine to thirty. The control plane goes first, and the API server must be at least as new as the kubelets; kubelets can lag by a few minor versions, which allows nodes to follow afterwards. Before starting, I read the release notes for deprecated and removed APIs, run a tool such as pluto or kubent to find manifests using them, check addon and CRD compatibility, and take an etcd backup. Then the nodes: I do a rolling upgrade, cordoning and draining each node or using surge nodes, which honours PodDisruptionBudgets so replicas stay available. Zero downtime depends on the workloads having multiple replicas spread across nodes, accurate readiness probes, graceful shutdown, and sensible PDBs. On managed clusters I use node pool surge upgrades with maxSurge and maxUnavailable. I upgrade a non-production cluster first, watch metrics throughout, and have a rollback plan, remembering that control plane downgrades are generally not supported.`,
    f: ["How do you find deprecated API usage before an upgrade?","What do surge upgrades on a managed node pool do?"]
  },
  94: {
    a: `etcd is a distributed key-value store using Raft consensus, and it holds all cluster state: every object, secret, and configuration. The API server is basically a typed front end over it, so etcd's health gates the whole control plane. For backup, I take a snapshot with etcdctl snapshot save, supplying the endpoint and client certificates, on a schedule, and store it encrypted and off-cluster. To restore, I stop the API servers and etcd, restore the snapshot into a new data directory with etcdctl snapshot restore, point etcd at it, restart, and verify. In a multi-member cluster, every member is restored from the same snapshot with new cluster settings. If etcd is unhealthy, the effects depend on quorum. With a majority of members, the cluster still works. Without quorum, the control plane goes read-only or unavailable: you can't create or change anything, but workloads already running continue. Slow disk is the commonest performance problem, so etcd needs fast SSDs, and I monitor fsync latency and database size, and defragment when needed. Managed Kubernetes handles all this for you.`,
    f: ["What happens to running pods when etcd loses quorum?","Why is disk latency so important for etcd?"]
  },
  95: {
    a: `Cluster cost is driven by node count, and node count is driven by pod requests, not actual usage, because the scheduler reserves capacity based on requests. If teams over-request, nodes look full while the CPU sits idle, and I pay for waste. If they under-request, nodes get overcommitted, causing contention, throttling, and OOM kills. So the first step is right-sizing: measure real usage with Prometheus and VPA recommendations, and set requests to roughly the ninety-fifth percentile plus headroom for CPU, and close to peak for memory, with limits chosen deliberately. Then bin-packing: aim for fewer, fuller nodes using a most-allocated scoring strategy or an autoscaler such as Karpenter with consolidation, choose instance sizes that fit the pod shapes well, and avoid fragmentation from very large pods. I keep a buffer for failures and spikes, since a cluster at ninety-five percent can't tolerate losing a node. Cost visibility with tools like OpenCost or Kubecost, labels for attribution, spot instances for fault-tolerant work, and regular reviews close the loop, with showback to teams so they own their requests.`,
    f: ["How do you choose requests for a spiky workload?","Why keep spare capacity rather than run nodes as full as possible?"]
  },
  96: {
    a: `GitOps makes Git the source of truth for desired cluster state, and an agent inside the cluster, Argo CD or Flux, continuously reconciles the live state toward the repository. That's a pull model. In traditional push-based CI/CD, the pipeline holds cluster credentials and runs kubectl apply or helm upgrade from outside. That creates risks: an external system holds production credentials, and there's no continuous drift detection, so manual changes go unnoticed. With pull-based GitOps, the cluster credentials never leave the cluster, CI only builds and pushes an image and updates a manifest or version in Git, and the agent detects drift and can automatically revert it with self-heal. Other benefits: Git history is the audit trail, and rollback is a git revert; every change goes through pull request review; and rebuilding a cluster is just pointing the agent at the repo. Costs to be aware of: managing secrets needs a pattern like External Secrets or Sealed Secrets, the repo structure needs design, and there's a feedback delay since you can't see deployment results directly from CI.`,
    f: ["How do you give CI feedback on whether a GitOps deploy succeeded?","How do you organise repositories for multiple environments?"]
  },
  97: {
    a: `Helm and Kustomize solve the multi-environment problem differently. Helm is templating plus packaging: a chart has templated YAML and a values file per environment, and it adds versioning, dependencies, releases, and rollback. It's good for distributing applications, like third-party software, and for heavy parameterisation. The downside is that Go templating inside YAML gets messy and hard to read. Kustomize is overlays with no templating: a base directory contains plain manifests, and per-environment overlays patch it declaratively with changes like replica counts, images, and resource limits. The result is always valid YAML and easy to review, and it's built into kubectl, but it's less suited to deep parameterisation or distribution as a package. In practice many teams use both: Helm for third-party charts, with values per environment, and Kustomize for their own applications, or Kustomize layered over rendered Helm output for last-mile patches. With GitOps tools, either works natively. My rule: start with Kustomize for internal apps for clarity, use Helm when I need to ship a configurable package or consume one, and avoid over-templating.`,
    f: ["When would you use Kustomize on top of a Helm chart?","What are the downsides of heavy Go templating?"]
  },
  98: {
    a: `A p99-only spike means a subset of requests are slow, so I'm hunting tail latency, not a general slowdown. I work from the application down. First, is it one pod or all? If one instance is slow, compare per-pod latency; an unhealthy pod or a noisy node would stand out, and load may be uneven. Second, CPU throttling: a pod with a CPU limit can be throttled by CFS even when average usage is low, hitting tail latency, visible in the throttled periods metric. Third, application causes: garbage collection pauses, lock contention, cold caches, or a slow code path for certain inputs. Fourth, dependencies: a tail of slow queries from the database or a slow downstream service, and retries that amplify it. Fifth, infrastructure: a node with disk or network trouble, noisy neighbours, and DNS timeouts affecting a fraction of lookups. I use distributed traces to compare slow and fast requests, and break down latency by endpoint, pod, and node. Fixes might be removing or raising CPU limits, adding timeouts and hedging, and fixing the slow dependency.`,
    f: ["Why does CPU throttling hurt p99 more than p50?","How do traces help isolate tail latency?"]
  },
  99: {
    a: `Multi-cluster means running workloads across several clusters, and I only do it when there's a clear reason. Reasons include surviving a region or cluster failure, serving users from the nearest region for latency, hard isolation between environments or teams for blast radius, scaling beyond a single cluster's limits, and data residency or compliance requirements. The cost is real: more to operate, upgrade, secure, and observe. For patterns, management is usually through GitOps: Argo CD or Flux managing a fleet from one repo, with ApplicationSets or app-of-apps generating per-cluster configuration. For traffic, global load balancing or DNS routes users to clusters, with health-based failover; a service mesh or Cilium Cluster Mesh can provide cross-cluster service discovery and mTLS; and there are multi-cluster Services APIs. For data, the hard part: stateful services need replication across regions, so I often keep state in a managed global database and treat clusters as stateless and replaceable. I also standardise cluster creation with Terraform or Cluster API so clusters are cattle, not pets.`,
    f: ["What makes state the hard part of multi-cluster?","When is a single larger cluster the better answer?"]
  },
  100: {
    a: `At scale, the API server and etcd become bottlenecks, and the symptoms are slow kubectl, lagging controllers, delayed scheduling, and clients getting throttled with 429 responses. Common causes include huge numbers or sizes of objects, such as giant ConfigMaps or Secrets and thousands of custom resources; expensive LIST calls without pagination, like listing all pods cluster-wide, that hammer etcd; a misbehaving controller or operator with a tight reconcile loop or a retry storm; slow etcd disks; and heavy watch churn or event volume. To investigate, I look at the API server metrics: request duration broken down by verb and resource, in-flight requests, and the API Priority and Fairness queues and rejections. The audit log and the user agent break down who is making the heavy calls. I check etcd metrics for fsync latency, database size, and leader changes. Fixes: stop or fix the noisy client, add pagination and watch caching, reduce object sizes and counts, clean up old Jobs and events, tune APF to protect critical traffic, speed up etcd storage, and shard workloads across clusters if the scale demands it.`,
    f: ["How do you find which client is overloading the API server?","What does API Priority and Fairness do?"]
  },
  101: {
    a: `Finalizers are pre-deletion hooks stored in metadata. When you delete an object that has finalizers, Kubernetes only sets a deletion timestamp; the object stays until each controller that owns a finalizer finishes its cleanup and removes its entry. Then the object is actually deleted. This lets controllers clean up external resources first, like a cloud load balancer or a disk. A resource gets stuck in Terminating when the controller responsible for the finalizer is gone or broken, so nobody removes it. A namespace sticks for similar reasons: it can't finish deleting until everything inside is gone, so a resource with a stuck finalizer, or an unavailable aggregated API service or webhook, blocks it. To debug, I look at the object's finalizers and its deletion timestamp, find which controller should handle them and check it is running, and for namespaces list the remaining resources and the namespace conditions. The last resort is manually patching the finalizers away, but that risks leaking external resources, such as orphaned cloud load balancers or disks, so I do it only after understanding what the finalizer was protecting.`,
    f: ["Why is removing a finalizer manually risky?","What can make a namespace stuck Terminating even when it looks empty?"]
  },
  102: {
    a: `Safe node maintenance depends on cordon, drain, and enough redundancy that losing a node is invisible to users. I cordon the node so nothing new schedules there, then drain it, using the Eviction API, which respects PodDisruptionBudgets, ignoring DaemonSets and deleting emptyDir data if appropriate. After the work I uncordon it. What makes it non-disruptive is the workload design: multiple replicas spread across nodes and zones with topology spread constraints; PDBs ensuring a minimum number stay available; a rolling strategy with maxUnavailable zero so new pods are Ready before old ones go; accurate readiness probes; and graceful shutdown with SIGTERM handling and preStop hooks so in-flight requests complete. For fleet-wide upgrades I prefer surge upgrades: add a new node first, then drain the old, so capacity never drops, or replace nodes immutably using new node images instead of patching in place. I do it one node or a small batch at a time, watch error rates, and schedule it away from peak traffic. I also verify that stateful pods can reattach their volumes in the right zone.`,
    f: ["What is a surge upgrade and why is it safer?","How do you handle a PDB that blocks the drain?"]
  },
  103: {
    a: `Distributed tracing follows one request through all the services it touches. The entry point creates a trace ID and a root span. Each service creates a child span for its own work and passes the trace context to the next service, usually in the W3C traceparent header. Each service sends its spans to a collector, and the backend assembles them into one trace, displayed as a waterfall showing where the time went. In Kubernetes I implement it with OpenTelemetry. I instrument applications with the OTel SDKs, using auto-instrumentation for many languages, or get some of it from a service mesh. An OpenTelemetry Collector runs as a DaemonSet or a gateway Deployment, receives spans, batches them, applies sampling, and exports them to Tempo, Jaeger, or a vendor. Context propagation is the important detail: a single service that drops the header breaks the trace, including through message queues, so I propagate it in message metadata too. I put the trace ID into log lines to link logs to traces, and use sampling, like tail-based sampling that keeps errors and slow traces, to control cost.`,
    f: ["Why do traces break across message queues, and how do you fix it?","What's the difference between head-based and tail-based sampling?"]
  },
  104: {
    a: `I separate CI, which produces a trusted artifact, from CD, which delivers it, ideally with GitOps. The CI stages are: lint and unit tests; build the image reproducibly from a pinned base, tagged with the Git commit SHA, never latest; scan it with Trivy or Grype and fail on critical vulnerabilities; generate an SBOM and sign the image with cosign; push to the registry and record the digest; and update the deployment manifest or Helm values with the new digest, committing to the GitOps repo. The CD stages: Argo CD or Flux detects the change and syncs the cluster; the rollout is progressive, with canary or blue-green using Argo Rollouts, gated on metrics; there are smoke tests and health checks after deploy; and automatic rollback if SLOs degrade. Promotion between environments is by promoting the same immutable digest, not rebuilding, with approvals before production. Around this I add security: OIDC for short-lived credentials, no cluster credentials in CI, policy checks at admission for signatures, and secrets from a manager. And I track DORA metrics: deployment frequency, lead time, change failure rate, and recovery time.`,
    f: ["Why promote the same image digest instead of rebuilding per environment?","What gates would you put between staging and production?"]
  },
  105: {
    a: `Mounting the Docker socket into a CI pod is a serious security risk, because anything that can talk to the Docker daemon is effectively root on the node and can start privileged containers. Docker-in-Docker avoids sharing the host socket but needs privileged mode, which is also insecure and slow. The alternative is daemonless builders. Kaniko builds from a Dockerfile inside an unprivileged container and pushes to the registry, with no daemon. Buildah builds OCI images without a daemon and can run rootless. BuildKit can run rootless and supports advanced caching and parallel builds, and is available as a standalone service. Cloud options like Cloud Build also isolate builds entirely. Practical considerations: use a layer cache stored in the registry to keep builds fast, run builds on dedicated, ephemeral node pools, isolated from production workloads, restrict network egress, and use short-lived credentials for pushing, via workload identity. I'd also scan and sign the resulting image. Kaniko has had limitations and maintenance concerns, so I check the current project status and consider BuildKit or Buildah as alternatives.`,
    f: ["Why is mounting the Docker socket equivalent to root on the node?","How do you speed up daemonless builds with caching?"]
  },
  106: {
    a: `Argo Rollouts replaces the Deployment controller with a Rollout resource that supports advanced strategies and automated analysis. In a canary strategy, I define steps: set the weight to ten percent, pause, run an analysis, go to fifty percent, then to one hundred. Traffic is shifted using an ingress controller or service mesh for precise weights, or just by replica counts without one. The analysis step queries metrics, like success rate from Prometheus, and if the SLO degrades, the rollout aborts and rolls back automatically. In a blue-green strategy, the full new version, the green stack, is started alongside the current blue one, can be tested through a preview service, and then traffic switches all at once by changing the active Service, with the old version kept briefly for instant rollback. The trade-offs: canary limits blast radius and validates with real traffic, but takes longer and needs good metrics and traffic splitting. Blue-green gives instant cutover and rollback but needs double capacity temporarily and exposes all users at the same moment. Either way, database changes must be backward compatible.`,
    f: ["What metrics would you use for automated canary analysis?","How does traffic splitting work without a service mesh?"]
  },
  107: {
    a: `I secure the pipeline end to end because each stage is a supply-chain entry point. Source: protected branches, required reviews, signed commits, and secret scanning so no credentials are in Git. Build: ephemeral, isolated runners, no Docker socket, pinned base images and dependencies, and scanning of code and images. Artifact: generate an SBOM, sign the image with cosign, push by digest, and use immutable tags. Deploy: use GitOps pull so CI holds no cluster credentials, and admission control verifies signatures and registry origin. Runtime: least-privilege service accounts, network policies, and runtime detection with Falco. Credentials get special attention: use OIDC federation so the pipeline obtains short-lived cloud tokens instead of long-lived secrets, scope them narrowly per job, and keep secrets in a secret manager, masked in logs. I also treat the CI system itself as critical infrastructure: restrict who can edit pipeline definitions, since changing the pipeline can exfiltrate secrets, control third-party actions by pinning them to commit SHAs, and keep audit logs. Finally, separate duties between building and approving production deploys.`,
    f: ["Why pin third-party actions to a commit SHA?","How does OIDC federation remove long-lived CI secrets?"]
  },
  108: {
    a: `In CI/CD I run helm upgrade with install, a values file per environment, and the image tag or digest set from the build, typically the Git commit SHA. The atomic flag is important: if the upgrade fails or times out, Helm automatically rolls back, and the wait flag with a timeout makes Helm wait for resources to become ready. Every upgrade stores a revision, kept in a Secret in the cluster, so helm history lists revisions and helm rollback goes back to a chosen one. Rollback re-applies the earlier manifests, but it can't undo non-Kubernetes side effects like database migrations, so schema changes need to be backward compatible. Also note that Helm doesn't upgrade CRDs in the crds directory, so those need separate handling. With GitOps through Argo CD or Flux, I don't usually run Helm from CI at all: the tool renders the chart from Git, and rollback means reverting the commit, which keeps Git the source of truth. I lint and template charts in CI, run helm diff before upgrades, and pin chart versions.`,
    f: ["Why doesn't helm rollback undo a database migration?","How are CRDs handled differently from other resources in Helm?"]
  },
  109: {
    a: `Running CI on Kubernetes means each job runs in a fresh pod instead of on a long-lived static runner. Tools include the GitHub Actions runner controller, GitLab's Kubernetes executor, Tekton, and Argo Workflows. The benefits are clean, isolated environments with no state bleeding between builds, elasticity, since pods scale to zero when idle and burst when the queue is long, using HPA or KEDA on queue depth, and efficient use of the existing cluster and spot nodes. The challenges: builds that need Docker require daemonless builders or sandboxing to avoid privileged containers; cold starts and image pulls slow jobs, so I use cached images and persistent or registry-backed build caches; resource contention can affect production if CI shares nodes, so I use dedicated node pools with taints; and security needs care, since CI pods run untrusted code, so I limit network access, service account permissions, and secrets. Debugging ephemeral pods is harder, so I keep logs and artifacts externally. For the right scale it's cost efficient, but small teams may be better off with a hosted runner service.`,
    f: ["How do you isolate CI workloads from production workloads?","How do you handle caching with ephemeral runners?"]
  },
  110: {
    a: `During a rolling update, old and new versions run at the same time against one database, so migrations must be backward-compatible. A destructive change, like dropping or renaming a column, breaks whichever version doesn't expect it, and I can't atomically swap the schema and every pod. The pattern is expand and contract, also called parallel change. Step one, expand: add the new column or table in a way both versions tolerate, for example nullable or with a default. Step two, deploy code that writes to both old and new, and can read either, and backfill existing data in batches. Step three, switch reads to the new structure. Step four, contract: after all old versions are gone and it's verified, remove the old column in a later release. Operationally I run migrations as a separate, controlled step, such as a Job or pipeline stage before the rollout, not in every pod's startup where replicas would race. I avoid long locks on big tables by using online schema change tools, and I keep migrations idempotent and tested, with a rollback or forward-fix plan.`,
    f: ["Why shouldn't every pod run migrations at startup?","How do you backfill a huge table without locking it?"]
  },
  111: {
    a: `Both revert to an earlier state, but they differ in how. kubectl rollout undo is imperative: it rolls the Deployment back to a previous ReplicaSet. It's fast, but it makes the live cluster diverge from Git, so the repository still contains the bad version. If a GitOps controller is running with self-heal, it will immediately re-apply the bad version from Git and undo your rollback, or the next deploy brings the bug back. A GitOps rollback is declarative: you git revert the bad commit and push. The controller syncs the cluster back, so the live state matches Git again, and the rollback is reviewed, recorded, and auditable. That's why it's safer and more consistent. The trade-off is speed: a GitOps rollback goes through the repository and sync interval, though a webhook and a manual sync make it quick. In an emergency I might pause auto-sync and use an imperative undo to stop the bleeding, but then immediately reconcile Git. Remember that neither undoes data changes such as migrations.`,
    f: ["What happens if you run kubectl rollout undo on an Argo CD managed app?","How do you make a GitOps rollback fast during an incident?"]
  },
  112: {
    a: `GPUs and similar devices aren't native Kubernetes resources, so the device plugin framework advertises them. A device plugin, such as NVIDIA's, runs as a DaemonSet on GPU nodes, discovers the devices, and registers them with the kubelet as an extended resource like nvidia.com slash gpu. Pods then request them in resource limits, for example one GPU, and the scheduler places them only on nodes with capacity. Extended resources are whole numbers and can't be overcommitted, so one GPU goes to one container, unless I use sharing features such as time slicing, MIG partitioning on supported NVIDIA cards, or MPS. For placement, I taint GPU nodes so ordinary pods don't land on expensive hardware, and give GPU pods a matching toleration and node affinity or selector for the GPU type. Operational points: install drivers and the container toolkit, often through the GPU Operator; autoscale GPU node pools carefully since they're expensive and slow to start; monitor GPU utilisation with DCGM exporter; and use spot capacity and queueing, with tools like Kueue, for batch training to improve utilisation.`,
    f: ["How can multiple pods share one GPU?","Why taint GPU nodes?"]
  },
  113: {
    a: `Good alerting pages a human only for problems that are urgent, actionable, and user-impacting. Bad alerts cause fatigue: cause-based alerts on transient conditions like a pod restarted once or CPU above eighty percent for one minute, alerts where there's nothing for the on-call person to do, per-pod noise in a fleet that heals itself, and static thresholds that flap. Good alerts are symptom-based: a breach of a user-facing SLO, using error rate or latency burn-rate, or a clear condition such as a Deployment with zero available replicas for five minutes. Each alert should link to a runbook and a dashboard and state what to check first. Cause-level signals, such as restarts, node pressure, and disk usage trends, go to dashboards or tickets, not pages. I also use alert hygiene: group and deduplicate in Alertmanager, add inhibition so a root cause suppresses downstream alerts, use for-durations to avoid blips, review noisy alerts after each on-call rotation, and delete alerts nobody acts on. The test is simple: if it pages me and I do nothing, it should not page.`,
    f: ["How does alert inhibition reduce noise?","What belongs in an alert's runbook?"]
  },
  114: {
    a: `Cilium uses eBPF to run networking, security, and load-balancing logic inside the Linux kernel instead of relying on long chains of iptables rules. It can replace kube-proxy entirely, doing Service load balancing in eBPF, which avoids the rule explosion and slow updates that iptables suffers at scale. For policy, it enforces NetworkPolicy using identities based on labels rather than IP addresses, so it handles pod churn efficiently, and it adds layer seven aware rules, such as allowing only GET on a given path, and DNS-aware policies like allowing egress to a specific domain. Hubble gives deep observability, showing flows, drops, and which policy dropped a packet, without sidecars. Teams adopt it for performance at scale, richer security, and visibility, plus extras like transparent encryption with WireGuard, cluster mesh for multi-cluster connectivity, and a sidecar-less service mesh. The trade-offs: it needs a reasonably modern kernel, it's a significant component to learn and operate, and debugging eBPF behaviour requires different tools than iptables. I'd adopt it when scale, policy needs, or observability justify the change.`,
    f: ["What advantage does identity-based policy have over IP-based policy?","What kernel requirements does Cilium impose?"]
  },
  115: {
    a: `Stateful disaster recovery needs two things: the data in another region, and the ability to rebuild the workload there. The data layer comes first, and the design is driven by RPO and RTO. For near-zero RPO I use application-level replication across regions, such as Postgres streaming or logical replication, or Kafka MirrorMaker, with a warm standby ready to be promoted. Volume snapshots replicated cross-region to object storage are cheaper but give a higher RPO. Velero backs up Kubernetes objects and volume snapshots and can restore them into another cluster. For the workload layer, the second region has a cluster built from the same Terraform and GitOps definitions, so it is reproducible, with the application manifests ready to apply, and DNS or a global load balancer to redirect traffic. The DR strategies range from backup and restore, which is cheapest and slowest, through pilot light and warm standby, to active-active, which is fastest and most complex and expensive. Whatever I choose, I document the failover runbook, automate what I can, and run regular DR drills, because untested DR is unreliable.`,
    f: ["How do you choose between warm standby and active-active?","What is the difference between RPO and RTO?"]
  },
  116: {
    a: `When someone says the cluster is slow, I narrow the scope before diving in. Is it kubectl and deploys that are slow, which points at the control plane, or are application responses slow, which points at the app, network, or node resources? One service or the whole cluster, one node or all? When did it start, and does it correlate with a deploy, a scale event, or an upgrade? Then I sweep the layers. Control plane: API server request duration, API Priority and Fairness throttling, and etcd fsync latency. Nodes: top nodes, disk and network saturation, node conditions, and pressure. Scheduling: Pending pods and autoscaler activity. Networking: DNS latency, kube-proxy and conntrack issues, and CNI health. Workloads: restarts, OOMKills, CPU throttling, and HPA behaviour. Application: golden signals, traces, and dependency latency. I use the USE method for resources and RED for services. Finally, I check recent changes first because they are the most likely cause, mitigate by rolling back or scaling, and document the findings.`,
    f: ["Which signals tell you the control plane is the bottleneck?","Why check recent changes first?"]
  },
  117: {
    a: `A Job runs pods until a number of them complete successfully, and a CronJob creates Jobs on a schedule. Reliability comes from the knobs. Completions and parallelism control how many successful runs are needed and how many run at once. backoffLimit sets the number of retries before the Job is marked failed, with exponential backoff. activeDeadlineSeconds is a hard time cap so a hung job can't run forever. ttlSecondsAfterFinished cleans up finished Jobs so they don't pile up. For CronJobs, concurrencyPolicy matters: Forbid skips a new run if the previous is still going, Replace cancels the old one, and Allow permits overlap, so I choose based on whether overlap is safe. startingDeadlineSeconds decides how late a missed run may still start, and I watch for the case where too many missed runs stop the schedule. Beyond that, the job itself must be idempotent, because retries and rare duplicate runs happen, with checkpoints for long work, proper resource requests, and restartPolicy OnFailure or Never. I alert on failed or missing runs, since silent batch failures are common.`,
    f: ["Why must batch jobs be idempotent?","How do you detect a CronJob that silently stopped running?"]
  },
  118: {
    a: `The Kubernetes scheduler makes a one-time decision when a pod is created and never moves it afterwards. Over time, nodes are added and drained, pods come and go, requests change, taints and labels change, so the original placement becomes suboptimal, with some nodes overloaded and others nearly empty, but nothing rebalances automatically. The Descheduler fills that gap. It runs as a Job or CronJob and evicts pods that violate configured strategies so the scheduler can place them better. Strategies include removing duplicate replicas from the same node, spreading pods that violate topology spread constraints, moving pods from over-utilised nodes to under-utilised ones, evicting pods that violate affinity or taints that have changed, and removing pods that restart too often or are too old. It respects PodDisruptionBudgets and skips pods without owners, such as bare pods, or critical ones. The risk is churn, since evicting pods causes disruption, so I tune thresholds conservatively and use it with good PDBs. Karpenter's consolidation does similar work for cost at the node level.`,
    f: ["Why does the descheduler not place pods itself?","What risks come with aggressive descheduling?"]
  },
  119: {
    a: `CRDs extend the Kubernetes API, and operators act on those resources, so at scale their versioning and upgrades become a real source of outages. CRDs support multiple versions, such as v1alpha1, v1beta1, and v1, with a conversion webhook between them. There's one stored version and several served versions, so migrating stored objects needs care, and the OpenAPI schema gates what users can submit. The pitfalls: Helm installs CRDs from the crds directory on first install only and does not upgrade them, so CRD changes are silently skipped and a new operator expects a schema that isn't there, so I manage CRDs separately, for example in their own chart or GitOps application. Deleting a CRD deletes all of its custom resources, which can destroy data, so I protect CRDs from accidental removal. A broken conversion webhook can break reads of the resource. Operators themselves need version compatibility with the CRD and the Kubernetes version. My approach is to pin versions, upgrade CRDs before operators, test upgrades in staging, read the release notes, back up the custom resources, and monitor operator health.`,
    f: ["Why is deleting a CRD dangerous?","How do you upgrade CRDs when using Helm?"]
  },
  120: {
    a: `I'd run a structured audit that produces a prioritised risk picture quickly, covering reliability, security, and cost. First, inventory and reliability: Kubernetes versions and whether they're end of life, upcoming forced upgrades, workloads without requests and limits, PDBs, or more than one replica, single points of failure like single-replica critical services or single-zone deployments, and whether etcd and stateful volumes are backed up and have ever been restored. Second, security posture: run kube-bench for the CIS benchmark, review RBAC for cluster-admin bindings and wildcard roles, check Pod Security settings, exposed services and dashboards, secrets handling, image sources and scanning, and network policies. Third, observability and operations: what monitoring, alerting, and logging exist, and is there a runbook or on-call process. Fourth, cost: compare requests with actual usage, find idle nodes and orphaned volumes and load balancers, and check autoscaling and instance choices. Then I rank findings by likelihood and impact, fix the quick wins and the dangerous ones first, and document the cluster as I go, using Infrastructure as Code to capture the state.`,
    f: ["What would you fix in the first week?","How do you capture an undocumented cluster's state into code?"]
  },
  121: {
    a: `A Pod is the smallest unit Kubernetes runs: one or more containers sharing network and storage. A ReplicaSet watches a label selector and makes sure a fixed number of identical Pods exist, recreating any that disappear. A Deployment sits above that. It owns ReplicaSets, and each change to the pod template creates a new ReplicaSet while the old one scales down, which is how rolling updates and rollbacks work. The chain is Deployment owns ReplicaSet owns Pod. I almost never create bare Pods in production because nothing recreates them if the node fails or someone drains it. The exceptions are throwaway debugging pods and static pods managed by the kubelet. For stateless apps I use a Deployment, for stable identity and storage a StatefulSet, for one per node a DaemonSet, and for batch work a Job. In an interview I would also mention revisionHistoryLimit, because old ReplicaSets are what rollback uses.`,
    f: ["Where is rollback history stored?","What does revisionHistoryLimit control?"]
  },
  122: {
    a: `QoS class is derived from the resource requests and limits on your containers, you do not set it directly. Guaranteed means every container sets CPU and memory requests equal to limits. Burstable means at least one request or limit is set but the pod does not meet the Guaranteed rule. BestEffort means nothing is set at all. It matters when a node runs short of memory or disk. The kubelet evicts BestEffort pods first, then Burstable pods that use more than they requested, and Guaranteed pods last. The same ordering feeds the Linux OOM score, so a Guaranteed pod is the last the kernel kills. For critical services I set requests equal to limits on memory at least, which gives predictable behaviour. For ordinary services I set realistic requests and a memory limit, and I am careful with CPU limits because they cause throttling.`,
    f: ["Why can CPU limits hurt latency?","How does the kubelet pick pods to evict?"]
  },
  123: {
    a: `A Namespace gives you a scope for names, RBAC and policies. ResourceQuota limits the aggregate: total CPU and memory requests, number of pods, services, PVCs, even object counts. LimitRange works at the individual object level. It sets default requests and limits, plus minimum and maximum values per container or pod, so a team cannot create a single huge pod or a pod with no limits. They work together. If a ResourceQuota covers CPU or memory, every new pod must specify them or be rejected, and a LimitRange default is what makes that painless. I use this for multi-team clusters: one namespace per team or environment, a quota sized to their budget, a LimitRange with sane defaults, and a NetworkPolicy plus RBAC on top. Namespaces alone are not a security boundary, so for hard isolation I would add policy engines or separate clusters.`,
    f: ["Is a namespace a security boundary?","What happens when a quota is exceeded?"]
  },
  124: {
    a: `ClusterIP is the default: a stable virtual IP reachable only inside the cluster, which is what service-to-service traffic uses. NodePort opens the same high port on every node and forwards to the Service, useful for simple tests or when an external load balancer is managed by hand. LoadBalancer builds on NodePort and asks the cloud provider to provision an external load balancer, so each Service gets its own cloud resource and cost. ExternalName is different, it creates a DNS CNAME to an outside hostname and does no proxying. In production I keep most Services as ClusterIP and expose a small number of entry points through an Ingress controller or Gateway API, so one load balancer serves many hosts and paths. I use a dedicated LoadBalancer Service for non-HTTP traffic such as TCP or UDP workloads.`,
    f: ["How does externalTrafficPolicy change the source IP?","When is a headless Service useful?"]
  },
  125: {
    a: `The kube-apiserver is the single entry point. It authenticates and authorises requests, runs admission, validates objects and persists them to etcd. etcd is the consistent key-value store that holds all cluster state. The kube-scheduler watches for unscheduled pods and picks a node by filtering and scoring. The kube-controller-manager runs the reconciliation loops, such as the ReplicaSet, Node, Job and EndpointSlice controllers, each pushing actual state toward desired state. The cloud-controller-manager handles cloud-specific work such as load balancers and node lifecycle. On every node the kubelet runs pods through the container runtime and reports status, and kube-proxy or an eBPF replacement programs Service routing. The key idea is that components only talk through the API server, so if something looks stuck I start by checking the API server and the relevant controller logs.`,
    f: ["What happens if the scheduler is down?","Which component creates the pods of a Deployment?"]
  },
  126: {
    a: `When a ConfigMap is injected as environment variables, the values are copied into the container at startup, so changing the ConfigMap does nothing until the pod is restarted. When it is mounted as a volume, the kubelet eventually updates the files, usually within a minute, using an atomic symlink swap. The catch is that the application must reload the file itself, and a subPath mount never updates at all. Because of this I choose based on the app. If it supports hot reload, I mount the volume. Otherwise I prefer immutable config and trigger a rollout whenever it changes, for example with a checksum annotation in the pod template or a tool such as Reloader. Making ConfigMaps immutable also reduces API server load and avoids accidental live changes. I treat config changes like code changes: versioned, reviewed and rolled out gradually.`,
    f: ["Why does subPath break updates?","What does an immutable ConfigMap give you?"]
  },
  127: {
    a: `Lifecycle hooks let you run a command or HTTP call at two points. postStart fires immediately after the container starts, but it runs alongside the main process and there is no guarantee it finishes first, so I avoid depending on it. preStop runs when the pod is being terminated, before the container receives SIGTERM, and it is the right place to stop accepting new work and drain in-flight requests. The sequence is: the pod is marked Terminating, it is removed from Service endpoints, preStop runs, SIGTERM is sent, and after terminationGracePeriodSeconds the kubelet sends SIGKILL. The grace period includes the preStop time. The classic production issue is that endpoint removal and shutdown race, so clients still hit a pod that is closing. A short sleep in preStop, plus an app that handles SIGTERM properly, usually fixes dropped requests during deployments.`,
    f: ["Why do users see 502s during a rollout?","What happens if preStop exceeds the grace period?"]
  },
  128: {
    a: `A Deployment stores its history as ReplicaSets. Every change to the pod template, such as a new image or env var, creates a new ReplicaSet with a revision annotation. Old ReplicaSets are kept at zero replicas, up to revisionHistoryLimit, which defaults to ten. kubectl rollout undo simply scales an earlier ReplicaSet back up and the current one down using the same rolling update strategy, so it is safe and gradual. Changing replica count alone does not create a revision, because it does not touch the template. Adding a change-cause annotation makes the history readable. One thing I always call out is GitOps: if Argo CD or Flux manages the Deployment, a manual undo drifts from Git and will be reverted, so the correct rollback is to revert the commit. Also remember that rollback does not undo database migrations.`,
    f: ["Does rollback restore ConfigMaps?","What if revisionHistoryLimit is zero?"]
  },
  129: {
    a: `A Helm chart packages Kubernetes manifests as Go templates. Chart.yaml holds metadata and dependencies, values.yaml holds defaults, and the templates folder holds the manifests plus helpers. A release is an installed instance of a chart, with its history stored as Secrets in the namespace. helm template renders the manifests locally without touching a cluster, which is ideal for linting, policy checks and reviewing what will be applied. helm upgrade with the install flag is the idempotent command pipelines use: it installs if the release does not exist and upgrades otherwise. The diff plugin compares the proposed release to what is live, which I want in every pull request. In CI I use atomic and a timeout so a failed upgrade rolls back automatically. For environments, I keep one chart with a values file per environment, and pin chart versions rather than using latest.`,
    f: ["Where does Helm store release state?","When would you choose Kustomize instead?"]
  },
  130: {
    a: `The original Endpoints object held every backend address for a Service in a single resource. In a Service with thousands of pods, each pod change rewrote that large object and pushed it to every node watching it, which created API server and network load. EndpointSlices fix this by splitting the addresses into multiple small objects, by default up to a hundred endpoints each. Only the affected slice is updated and sent. They also carry richer data such as ready, serving and terminating conditions, node name and topology zone, which supports features like topology-aware routing and graceful termination. kube-proxy, Cilium and most ingress controllers now consume EndpointSlices. For day-to-day debugging I still check that a Service has endpoints, and if the list is empty I look at the selector, the pod labels and readiness.`,
    f: ["What does it mean if a Service has no endpoints?","How does topology-aware routing use slices?"]
  },
  131: {
    a: `A kubeconfig file contains three lists: clusters with their API endpoints and CA, users with their credentials, and contexts that tie a cluster, a user and a default namespace together. The risk with several clusters is running a command against the wrong one. My habits are to show the current context and namespace in the shell prompt, colour production differently, pass the context explicitly in scripts with the context flag, and keep separate kubeconfig files per environment selected with the KUBECONFIG variable. For authentication I avoid long-lived static tokens and use the cloud provider's exec plugin or OIDC login so credentials are short-lived and tied to a person. I also use RBAC so day-to-day access to production is read-only, with elevated access granted just in time. Tools like kubectx help, but the safeguard is process, not tooling.`,
    f: ["How do you give temporary admin access?","Why avoid static tokens in kubeconfig?"]
  },
  132: {
    a: `kubectl debug node creates a pod scheduled on a specific node, with the host's root filesystem mounted under slash host and host namespaces available. From there I can read kubelet and container runtime logs, inspect disk usage, check iptables or conntrack, and chroot into the host if I need host tools. Compared with SSH, there is no need to open port 22 or manage keys, access is authorised through Kubernetes RBAC, and every action starts with an auditable API call. It is also often the only option on managed or immutable node images, such as Bottlerocket or Talos, where SSH is disabled by design. Because the pod is privileged, I restrict who can create it, clean it up when done, and avoid making changes by hand on nodes. Anything I learn goes back into automation or node images.`,
    f: ["What RBAC permission does this need?","How would you debug a node on Talos?"]
  },
  133: {
    a: `Static pods are pods the kubelet manages straight from manifest files in a configured directory, typically slash etc slash kubernetes slash manifests. The kubelet watches the folder, starts the pod, restarts it if it dies, and creates a read-only mirror pod in the API server so it is visible with kubectl. Because they do not depend on the control plane, they solve the bootstrap problem. In a kubeadm cluster the API server, controller manager, scheduler and etcd all run as static pods. To change an API server flag, I edit the manifest on that node and the kubelet recreates the pod, and kubectl edit on the mirror pod will not work. I also use static pods rarely for node-critical agents. The trade-off is that they are not managed by a controller, so there is no rolling update or central management.`,
    f: ["Why can't you delete a static pod with kubectl?","How would you upgrade control plane flags safely?"]
  },
  134: {
    a: `The kubelet runs on every node. It watches for pods assigned to its node, asks the runtime to pull images and start containers, runs probes, mounts volumes, reports status and enforces resource limits. It does not run containers itself. It speaks the Container Runtime Interface, a gRPC API, to a runtime such as containerd or CRI-O, and those in turn use a low-level runtime such as runc to create the actual Linux containers. Kubernetes removed the built-in Docker integration, dockershim, in version 1.24, but images built with Docker are standard OCI images and still run. For debugging on a node I use crictl, since the docker command may not exist. The usual failure points are a stopped kubelet, a full disk, runtime socket problems, or certificate expiry, so I check journalctl for the kubelet first.`,
    f: ["What is the difference between containerd and runc?","How do you list containers on a node without Docker?"]
  },
  135: {
    a: `Block storage such as EBS or a persistent disk lives in a single availability zone. With Immediate binding, Kubernetes provisions the volume as soon as the PVC is created, before any pod exists, so the scheduler may later be forced to place the pod in a different zone than the disk, leaving the pod stuck Pending with a volume node affinity conflict. WaitForFirstConsumer delays provisioning until a pod that uses the claim is scheduled. The scheduler then considers all the pod's constraints, picks a node, and the volume is created in that node's zone. It matters for multi-zone clusters and StatefulSets. A side effect is that the PVC stays Pending until a pod consumes it, which is expected. I set this mode on every zonal StorageClass, and combine it with topology spread so replicas land in different zones.`,
    f: ["What is a volume node affinity conflict?","How does this interact with pod anti-affinity?"]
  },
  136: {
    a: `Access modes describe how many nodes can use a volume at once, and what they can do. ReadWriteOnce means a single node can mount it read-write. Note that it is per node, not per pod, so multiple pods on the same node can share it. ReadOnlyMany lets many nodes mount it read-only. ReadWriteMany lets many nodes mount it read-write, but block devices cannot do this, so you need a shared file system such as NFS, EFS, Azure Files or CephFS. ReadWriteOncePod restricts it to exactly one pod in the whole cluster and is the right choice when you need strict single-writer safety. The storage backend must support the mode you request or the claim stays Pending. In design reviews I ask whether the app really needs shared writes, because RWX file systems are slower and often the wrong tool for databases.`,
    f: ["Why does a Deployment with RWO and rolling updates sometimes hang?","Which mode suits a database?"]
  },
  137: {
    a: `The reclaim policy decides what happens to the storage when the claim bound to a PersistentVolume is deleted. With Delete, which is the default for dynamically provisioned volumes, the cloud disk is destroyed along with the PVC. That is convenient for dev and test but dangerous in production, because deleting a namespace or a StatefulSet's claims can destroy data. With Retain, the PV moves to a Released state and the disk stays, so an administrator can recover the data, but the volume is not automatically reused and needs manual cleanup or a new PV that references it. For production databases I set Retain on the StorageClass, or patch the PV, and add snapshots and tested restores, since Retain protects against accidental deletion, not corruption or region failure. I also enable protection finalizers so an in-use PVC cannot be removed.`,
    f: ["How do you reuse a Released volume?","What does the storage-object-in-use protection do?"]
  },
  138: {
    a: `The Ingress resource is just configuration: hosts, paths and which Service each should go to. By itself it does nothing. An Ingress controller is the running component that watches Ingress objects and programs a real proxy or cloud load balancer, and the IngressClass says which controller owns which Ingress. For TLS, the Ingress references a Secret of type kubernetes.io/tls containing the certificate and key, and the controller terminates HTTPS and forwards to pods, often over plain HTTP inside the cluster or re-encrypted if required. I normally automate certificates with cert-manager and Let's Encrypt or a private CA. Ingress is limited to HTTP and relies on annotations for advanced features, so newer designs use the Gateway API, which has clearer roles and richer routing. For TCP or UDP I use a LoadBalancer Service.`,
    f: ["Why is Gateway API replacing Ingress?","How do you do mTLS at the edge?"]
  },
  139: {
    a: `cert-manager adds custom resources for certificates. An Issuer or ClusterIssuer describes who signs, for example Let's Encrypt through ACME, HashiCorp Vault, or an internal CA. A Certificate resource states the names you need and the Secret to store them in. You can also annotate an Ingress and cert-manager creates the Certificate for you. For ACME it creates an Order and Challenges. HTTP-01 proves control by serving a token over port 80, DNS-01 proves it by creating a DNS record, which also supports wildcard certificates. Once issued, the key and certificate go in a Secret, which Ingress or workloads mount. cert-manager renews automatically, by default when about a third of the lifetime remains. When it fails, I look at the Certificate, CertificateRequest, Order and Challenge events in that order, and alert on expiry as a safety net.`,
    f: ["When would you choose DNS-01?","How do pods pick up a renewed cert?"]
  },
  140: {
    a: `Labels are key-value pairs used to group and select objects: Services pick pods, Deployments own ReplicaSets, and policies target workloads, all by label selectors. Annotations hold metadata that tools use but that is never selected on, for example ingress settings, checksums or build information. My practices are to adopt the standard app.kubernetes.io labels for name, instance, version, component and part-of, add ownership and environment labels for cost allocation, keep selector labels minimal and stable because a Deployment's selector is immutable, and avoid putting changing values like the version in the selector. I never store secrets in either, and I keep annotation size modest. A common production bug is overlapping selectors, where a Service or Deployment accidentally adopts pods it should not own, so I review selectors carefully and use unique names per workload.`,
    f: ["Why is a Deployment selector immutable?","How would you find all pods by team for cost reports?"]
  },
  141: {
    a: `Traditionally a sidecar was just another container in the pod's container list. That had two problems: there was no start ordering, so the app could start before the proxy was ready, and in a Job the pod never finished because the sidecar kept running. Native sidecars, enabled by default in recent Kubernetes versions, are declared as init containers with restartPolicy Always. They start in order before the main containers, stay running for the life of the pod, support their own probes, and are terminated after the main containers exit. That makes service mesh proxies, log shippers and credential refreshers behave properly, and lets batch Jobs complete. I would use a sidecar when the helper must share the pod's network or volumes and have the same lifecycle, and choose a separate Deployment or DaemonSet when it is independent.`,
    f: ["How is this different from a regular init container?","What happens to the sidecar at pod shutdown?"]
  },
  142: {
    a: `CPU requests set the share a container gets under contention, and the scheduler uses them for placement. A CPU limit is a hard cap enforced by the Linux CFS quota: the container may use a certain amount of CPU time per hundred millisecond period, and once it is spent the threads are paused until the next period. For multi-threaded or bursty applications this means you can be throttled even though the node has idle CPU, which shows up as latency spikes. You can see it in the container CPU throttled periods metric. Because of this, many teams set accurate CPU requests and no CPU limit, letting pods burst into spare capacity, while keeping memory limits because memory cannot be reclaimed safely. The trade-off is noisy neighbours, so I keep requests honest, use Guaranteed class for critical pods, and monitor node saturation.`,
    f: ["How do you measure throttling?","What is the downside of having no CPU limit?"]
  },
  143: {
    a: `By default pods accept traffic from anywhere. To get zero trust I first apply a policy per namespace with an empty pod selector and policy types set to both Ingress and Egress, with no rules, which denies everything. The first thing that breaks is DNS, because egress to CoreDNS is blocked, so I immediately add an egress rule that allows UDP and TCP port 53 to the kube-dns pods in kube-system, selected with a namespace selector and a pod label. Then I add explicit allow policies per application: the frontend can reach the API, the API can reach the database, and the ingress controller can reach the frontend. I test each step with a debug pod. It is vital to check that the CNI actually enforces policies, since some do not, and to remember that policies are additive.`,
    f: ["How do you allow traffic to a cloud metadata service?","How do you test policies before enforcing them?"]
  },
  144: {
    a: `Audit logging records requests handled by the API server, each as a structured event with the user, groups, source IP, verb, resource, namespace, response code and time. An audit policy decides what is recorded and at which level: None, Metadata, Request, or RequestResponse. I record Metadata for most things, log secrets only at Metadata so values never reach the logs, and capture more detail for RBAC changes and exec calls. The output goes to a file or a webhook, and from there to a log platform. In an incident I answer questions like who deleted this deployment, who created this cluster role binding, which service account listed secrets, and from which IP. On EKS, GKE and AKS I turn on the managed control plane audit logs, since they are not always enabled. Alerts on exec into pods, privileged pod creation and RBAC changes are valuable.`,
    f: ["Why not log secrets at RequestResponse?","Which events would you alert on?"]
  },
  145: {
    a: `metrics-server is a lightweight aggregator. It scrapes the kubelet's resource metrics endpoint, keeps the latest CPU and memory numbers in memory, and exposes them through the metrics API. That is what kubectl top and the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler read. It stores no history and has no application metrics, so it cannot support dashboards or alerting. Prometheus is a full monitoring system: it scrapes targets such as kubelet cAdvisor, kube-state-metrics, node exporters and application endpoints, stores time series, and lets you query with PromQL and fire alerts. For custom or external metrics the HPA needs an adapter such as the Prometheus adapter or KEDA. In production you need both: metrics-server for autoscaling and quick checks, Prometheus for history and alerts. If kubectl top returns an error I check that metrics-server is running and that its APIService is available, then look at kubelet TLS settings.`,
    f: ["Why does the HPA show unknown for metrics?","What does kube-state-metrics add?"]
  },
  146: {
    a: `The standard HPA scales a workload from resource metrics like CPU and memory, or custom metrics if you add an adapter, and it needs at least one replica. KEDA is an operator that adds event-driven autoscaling. You define a ScaledObject that points at a trigger such as SQS or RabbitMQ queue depth, Kafka consumer lag, a Prometheus query or a cron schedule. KEDA creates and manages an HPA behind the scenes and also handles the zero-to-one activation, so idle workers can scale to zero and cost nothing. I use it for queue consumers and batch workers where load is driven by backlog rather than CPU. I avoid scale to zero for latency-sensitive services because of cold start. Important settings are cooldown periods, polling interval and a sensible maximum replica count so a burst does not overwhelm downstream systems.`,
    f: ["What happens to in-flight messages at scale-down?","How do you cap downstream load?"]
  },
  147: {
    a: `I start with the node condition and events to confirm DiskPressure, then check which filesystem is affected: the node root, or the image and container storage. Using a debug pod I run du on the container runtime directory and var log to see what is large. Usual culprits are unrotated container logs, a pile of unused images, pods writing big files to emptyDir or their writable layer, and core dumps. Immediate relief is to prune unused images, remove old logs, or cordon and drain the node and replace it. Long term I set container log rotation in the kubelet, set ephemeral-storage requests and limits so a misbehaving pod is evicted rather than the node suffering, tune image garbage collection thresholds, and move heavy temporary data to a proper volume. I also alert on node disk usage before eviction thresholds, so we act at eighty percent, not at the edge.`,
    f: ["What are the kubelet eviction thresholds?","How do ephemeral-storage limits help?"]
  },
  148: {
    a: `First I look at the pod's deletion timestamp, finalizers, node and events. If the node is NotReady or unreachable, the kubelet cannot report the container as gone, so the pod stays Terminating until the node returns or is removed. If there are finalizers, some controller has not finished its cleanup, so I find that controller and fix it rather than removing the finalizer blindly. If the node is healthy, the application may be ignoring SIGTERM, with a long terminationGracePeriodSeconds, or a volume unmount is hanging, which I see in kubelet logs. Force deleting the pod object removes it from the API but does not guarantee the process is dead, which is dangerous for StatefulSets and volumes. So I only force delete after confirming the node or process is gone. Prevention is handling SIGTERM correctly and keeping sensible grace periods.`,
    f: ["Why is force delete risky for StatefulSets?","How do you find which controller owns a finalizer?"]
  },
  149: {
    a: `The key clue is that the same image works elsewhere, so I focus on what differs per node. I look at which nodes fail and read the pod events for the exact error. A rate limit message points to anonymous Docker Hub pulls shared by a NAT address, so I add imagePullSecrets or a registry mirror. An unauthorized error suggests node credentials, such as a missing ECR policy on one node group's IAM role. A timeout or no such host suggests DNS, proxy, security group or route problems on that subnet. I reproduce on the node using crictl pull, and check disk space, since a full image filesystem also fails pulls. The fix is usually to standardise node roles and networking, use a pull-through cache in our own registry, and pin images by digest so behaviour is consistent.`,
    f: ["How do you avoid Docker Hub limits at scale?","Why pin images by digest?"]
  },
  150: {
    a: `I walk from the inside out. First confirm the Service has ready endpoints, because a selector or readiness problem would also break internal access. Since the ClusterIP works, the pods are probably fine, so the problem is the external path. I test the NodePort from another machine on the same network, then from the node itself. If it works locally but not remotely, it is a cloud security group, network ACL or firewall blocking the NodePort range. If one node works and another does not, I check externalTrafficPolicy: with Local, only nodes running a backend pod respond, and the load balancer health check should exclude the others. I also check kube-proxy is running and its rules exist. The fix is opening the right security group rule or switching traffic policy, and for the long term I prefer a managed load balancer or Ingress over raw NodePorts.`,
    f: ["What does externalTrafficPolicy Cluster do to the client IP?","How does a load balancer health check work with Local?"]
  },
  151: {
    a: `This is the classic self-inflicted outage. An admission webhook, for example from Gatekeeper, Kyverno or a service mesh injector, is called by the API server on matching requests. If the webhook pods are down, or the serving certificate expired, and the failure policy is Fail, every matching request is rejected, including the request that would recreate the webhook pods, which creates a deadlock. To recover I list the webhook configurations, find the one whose errors appear in the API server message, and temporarily delete it or set its failure policy to Ignore, then fix the underlying certificate or deployment and restore it. To prevent a repeat, I exclude system namespaces and the webhook's own namespace using namespace selectors, set short timeouts, run multiple replicas with disruption budgets, automate certificate rotation, and alert on webhook latency and error rates.`,
    f: ["When is failurePolicy Fail appropriate?","How do you roll out a new policy safely?"]
  },
  152: {
    a: `When the etcd database passes its quota, it raises a NOSPACE alarm and the cluster stops accepting writes, so the API server becomes effectively read-only. First I take a snapshot for safety. Then I compact the key history to the latest revision, which marks old versions as free, defragment each member one at a time to actually return the space, and finally disarm the alarm. Defrag blocks the member while it runs, so I do it sequentially, followers first. Then I investigate the root cause. Common causes are a huge number of Events, a controller or operator that updates objects in a loop, large Secrets or CRs, or leftover Jobs. I fix the source, enable automatic compaction, set an alert on database size, and, if needed, raise the quota modestly. On managed clusters I would open a support ticket because I cannot reach etcd directly.`,
    f: ["Why isn't compaction enough on its own?","How do you spot which resource type is largest?"]
  },
  153: {
    a: `ContainerCreating means the scheduler has done its job and the kubelet is preparing the pod. I go straight to the pod events because they usually give the reason. FailedMount or FailedAttachVolume points to storage: a missing PVC, a volume still attached to another node, a missing Secret or ConfigMap, or a CSI driver problem. FailedCreatePodSandBox often means the CNI plugin could not set up networking, for example because the node ran out of IP addresses or the CNI pod is unhealthy. Slow progress with pulling messages suggests registry or bandwidth problems with large images. If events are quiet, I read kubelet and container runtime logs on the node. Once I know the layer, I fix it, such as correcting the object name, freeing the volume, or replacing a node with exhausted IPs. I would also add alerts for pods stuck longer than a few minutes.`,
    f: ["What does FailedCreatePodSandBox usually indicate?","How do you check if a CSI driver is healthy?"]
  },
  154: {
    a: `Inside a pod, resolv.conf has several search domains and ndots set to five. Any name with fewer than five dots is first tried with each search suffix, so looking up an external host like api.example.com can trigger several failed queries before the right one, and each failure adds load and latency. If CoreDNS is overloaded, or there is a conntrack race on UDP, a lost packet causes a classic five second timeout. I check CoreDNS CPU and logs, its cache and forward settings, and the resolv.conf. Fixes include using fully qualified names with a trailing dot for external calls, lowering ndots with dnsConfig for chatty workloads, scaling CoreDNS with the cluster proportional autoscaler, and deploying NodeLocal DNSCache so lookups hit a local cache over TCP. I would also measure with dnsperf style tests to confirm the improvement.`,
    f: ["What is NodeLocal DNSCache?","Why does the UDP race cause 5 second delays?"]
  },
  155: {
    a: `The AWS VPC CNI gives each pod a secondary private IP from the node's elastic network interfaces. The instance type limits how many interfaces and addresses it can hold, so it limits the pods per node, and subnets with small CIDR blocks can be exhausted as the cluster grows, leaving pods in ContainerCreating with IP allocation errors. I check the aws-node logs and subnet free address counts. Remedies include enabling prefix delegation, which assigns blocks of sixteen addresses per slot and greatly increases density, using custom networking so pods draw from a secondary CIDR such as the 100.64 range, adding larger subnets, or choosing an overlay or Cilium based networking. I would also right-size the number of nodes and consider IPv6. Long term, I plan pod CIDR capacity at design time and alert when subnets drop below a free address threshold.`,
    f: ["What is prefix delegation?","How does an overlay network avoid this?"]
  },
  156: {
    a: `A block volume with ReadWriteOnce can be attached to only one node. When a node dies without cleanly detaching, the VolumeAttachment object still says the disk belongs to it, so when the pod is rescheduled to a new node, the attach fails with a Multi-Attach error. Kubernetes eventually force-detaches after a timeout, around six minutes, once the node is considered unhealthy, and newer versions with non-graceful node shutdown handling can speed that up. Manually, I first confirm the old node is really down or terminated, preferably by stopping or deleting the instance, because if it is still running and writing, detaching risks data corruption. Then I delete the stale VolumeAttachment or the node object so the volume can attach to the new node. For better resilience I use replicated storage or databases with their own replication, instead of depending on one disk.`,
    f: ["What is non-graceful node shutdown?","How would a StatefulSet recover faster?"]
  },
  157: {
    a: `First I watch the HPA events and the metric values to see what triggers each decision. Flapping typically has a few causes. The target utilisation may sit right at the normal operating point, so small changes cross the threshold. The metric may be spiky, like instantaneous CPU. New pods may take long to start, so the average drops, then the HPA scales down, and load rises again. To stabilise I use the behavior section: a longer scale-down stabilization window of five minutes, policies limiting how many pods can be removed per minute, and faster scale-up. I choose a steadier metric, such as requests per second or queue depth, set the CPU requests accurately since utilisation is relative to requests, make sure readiness probes reflect true readiness, and set minReplicas high enough to absorb normal load. Then I test with load to verify.`,
    f: ["Why is CPU utilisation relative to requests?","What does stabilizationWindowSeconds do?"]
  },
  158: {
    a: `The 502s mean requests reach a pod that cannot answer. There are two directions. On the way up, a pod may be marked Ready too early, so I make sure the readiness probe checks real dependencies and warm-up, and use a startup probe for slow apps. On the way down, endpoint removal and SIGTERM happen at the same time, so load balancers and the proxy keep sending requests for a few seconds. The fix is a short sleep in a preStop hook, an application that stops accepting new connections and finishes in-flight requests on SIGTERM, and a grace period longer than the longest request. I set maxUnavailable to zero with a surge, add a PodDisruptionBudget, and align load balancer deregistration delay and keep-alive timeouts with the application. Then I verify under load during a rollout, not just in a quiet test.`,
    f: ["Why does SIGTERM race with endpoint removal?","What should maxUnavailable be for a critical service?"]
  },
  159: {
    a: `I begin with a pending pod's events. If I see NotTriggerScaleUp, the message lists why each node group was rejected. Typical reasons are that the node group is already at its maximum size, that the pod requests more CPU or memory than the largest instance type, that node selectors, tolerations or zone constraints match no group, or that a PVC bound to one zone cannot use a group in another zone. Then I read the autoscaler logs and status ConfigMap. Next I check outside Kubernetes: cloud quotas, insufficient capacity for the instance type, launch template errors, and IAM permissions or tags that let the autoscaler discover the group. The fix depends on the cause, such as raising the maximum, adding more instance types, correcting requests, or moving to Karpenter which provisions right-sized nodes directly. I add an alert on pods pending longer than a few minutes.`,
    f: ["How is Karpenter different in this situation?","Why can PVC zones block scale up?"]
  },
  160: {
    a: `My first action is to revoke and rotate the credential, because once a secret has been pushed I assume it is exposed, even if the repository is private. Next I limit the blast radius: find what the secret grants access to and review the provider's access logs and the cluster audit logs for suspicious use since the commit time. Then I replace it in the cluster with the new value and roll out the workloads that use it. Cleaning history with git filter-repo is good hygiene, but it is not the fix. After the incident I address the root cause: add pre-commit and pipeline secret scanning, stop storing plain secrets in manifests by using External Secrets Operator, Sealed Secrets, or SOPS with a cloud key management service, prefer workload identity over static keys, and write a short post-incident review so the team learns without blame.`,
    f: ["How does External Secrets Operator work?","Why prefer workload identity over keys?"]
  },
  161: {
    a: `The forbidden message is precise: it states which user cannot perform which verb on which resource in which namespace. For exec the resource is the pods/exec subresource with the create verb, which is easy to miss when someone granted only get, list and watch on pods. I confirm with kubectl auth can-i using impersonation, and list everything the user can do with the list flag. Then I check which RoleBindings and ClusterRoleBindings reference the user or their groups. With single sign-on, I check that the group claim in the token matches the subject name in the binding, as a mismatch is a frequent cause. I then grant the least privilege needed, ideally a Role limited to the namespace and expiring access, and document it in code. For production, I might not allow exec at all, and instead provide ephemeral debug containers with audit.`,
    f: ["Why is pods/exec a sensitive permission?","How do group claims reach RBAC?"]
  },
  162: {
    a: `Linux tracks every connection through iptables in a fixed size table. When the table fills, new packets are dropped, which shows up as random timeouts, and the kernel logs table full. On busy Kubernetes nodes this is often caused by many short-lived connections, such as services without keep-alive, high-volume DNS over UDP, or NAT heavy traffic through Services. I check the current count against the maximum and look at which workloads open the most connections. A quick fix is raising nf_conntrack_max and the hash size with sysctl, making sure the node has the memory for it. Real solutions reduce churn: enable connection reuse, add NodeLocal DNSCache, lower unnecessary timeouts, and spread chatty pods across nodes. Cilium with kube-proxy replacement reduces dependence on conntrack. I also add node-level monitoring so we see it coming.`,
    f: ["Why does DNS over UDP stress conntrack?","How does eBPF reduce conntrack usage?"]
  },
  163: {
    a: `I plot container working set memory per pod over several days and compare it with the memory limit. A leak shows as a rising baseline after each garbage collection or restart, and eventually OOMKilled restarts, visible in the last terminated state. I make sure I'm using working set bytes, because that is the figure the kernel cgroup limit applies to, rather than cache-inclusive usage. I correlate the growth with traffic, deployments and specific endpoints to see what triggers it. Then I move to application level evidence: language runtime metrics like heap size and GC time, heap profiles or dumps taken from a canary, tools like pprof, async-profiler or memory profilers, and look for unbounded caches, goroutine or thread leaks, and unclosed connections. As a mitigation I set a right-sized limit, add a restart policy, and alert on the growth slope, while the code fix is worked on.`,
    f: ["Why working set rather than usage?","What else besides heap can exceed the limit?"]
  },
  164: {
    a: `Prometheus holds the recent data and the index of all active series in memory, so memory grows with series count, which is the product of metrics, label combinations and targets. I start with the TSDB status endpoint or promtool analyze to list the metric names and labels with the most series. The usual cause is a label with unbounded values: user IDs, request paths with IDs inside, pod names in a high churn environment, or histogram buckets multiplied by many labels. Fixes are to remove or normalise those labels in the application, use metric relabeling or drop rules at scrape time, lower the number of scraped targets and histogram buckets, and shorten retention on the local store. For scale I shard by team with several Prometheus instances, use remote write to Thanos, Mimir or a managed service, and enforce series limits per scrape so one team cannot break monitoring.`,
    f: ["What is cardinality, in one sentence?","How do series limits protect Prometheus?"]
  },
  165: {
    a: `I start with the app diff to see exactly which fields differ. A permanent OutOfSync is often caused by something else mutating the resource: an HPA changing replicas, a mutating webhook injecting sidecars, or API server defaulting that Git does not include. For those I configure ignoreDifferences or respect-ignore-differences rather than fighting it. If the app is Progressing, I look at the resource health tree to see which object is not healthy, such as a Deployment waiting for pods, a Job from a PreSync hook that never completes, or a custom resource without a health check defined. I read the operation state and controller logs for sync errors like invalid manifests, missing CRDs or permission problems. Fixes include correcting the manifest, ordering resources using sync waves, adding custom health checks, and setting retry limits. I also keep a sync window and alerts so problems surface early.`,
    f: ["What are sync waves and hooks?","Why would an HPA cause OutOfSync?"]
  },
  166: {
    a: `Helm records each release revision as a Secret, and if an upgrade is interrupted, for example when the pipeline is cancelled or the connection drops, the latest revision can remain in a pending state, and later upgrades report that another operation is in progress. I check the release history to see the revisions and statuses. The preferred recovery is helm rollback to the last good deployed revision, which creates a new revision and clears the problem. If rollback is blocked, I carefully delete the specific stuck revision Secret in the namespace, after backing it up, then rerun the upgrade. Before retrying I find out why the first upgrade failed, using helm template and dry run, and look at pod events. To prevent a repeat I use atomic with a timeout, avoid cancelling deployment jobs, use a concurrency lock in CI, and run helm diff in review.`,
    f: ["Where does Helm store release data?","What does --atomic do on failure?"]
  },
  167: {
    a: `Kubernetes schedules a pod only when it is created. After a scale-down, a node failure or a zone event, the replicas that survive stay where they are, so a service can end up concentrated in one zone, which defeats high availability. To fix the current state I can do a rollout restart so the scheduler places new pods with the right constraints, or run the descheduler to evict pods violating spread rules. To prevent it, I add topologySpreadConstraints on the zone and hostname keys with a maxSkew of one, using DoNotSchedule for strict workloads or ScheduleAnyway for flexible ones, together with enough capacity in every zone. I also use PodDisruptionBudgets so draining nodes does not remove too many replicas at once, and make sure the autoscaler has node groups in every zone. Then I test by simulating a zone loss.`,
    f: ["What does maxSkew mean?","When would you not use DoNotSchedule?"]
  },
  168: {
    a: `I look at the ingress controller logs to see which status, upstream and timing are involved. A 504 means the proxy waited for the upstream longer than its read timeout, 60 seconds by default in NGINX. I check whether the backend is genuinely slow, for example a heavy query or exhausted connection pool, whether pods are overloaded, and whether the cloud load balancer in front of the ingress has its own idle timeout that is shorter. Raising the proxy read timeout for a specific long-running endpoint is acceptable, but I prefer making long operations asynchronous. A 413 means the request body is larger than the proxy body size limit, one megabyte by default in NGINX, so uploads need a higher limit on that Ingress. I change these on specific Ingress objects through annotations, not globally, and I make sure the application and every hop agree.`,
    f: ["Where else can a timeout be set in the path?","How would you handle large uploads?"]
  },
  169: {
    a: `Before the upgrade I inventory which API versions I use. The API server exposes a metric that counts requests to deprecated APIs, and tools like Pluto and kubent scan live clusters, Helm releases and manifests in Git for removed versions. I read the release notes and deprecation guide for each minor version in the path, since the upgrade must go one minor at a time. I convert the manifests to supported versions, update Helm charts and operators, and check the add-ons such as the CNI, CSI drivers, ingress controller, metrics-server and service mesh for compatibility with the target version. Then I upgrade a non-production cluster first and run smoke tests. For production I back up etcd, upgrade the control plane, then roll the nodes gradually with surge nodes and PodDisruptionBudgets. A written runbook with rollback criteria reduces risk.`,
    f: ["Why only one minor version at a time?","How do you test add-on compatibility?"]
  },
  170: {
    a: `For a Job I read its status, the pod events and logs. If pods repeatedly fail, the backoff limit eventually marks the Job failed, so I fix the cause. If the Job stays active, the main container may not exit, or a sidecar such as a proxy never terminates, which the native sidecar feature solves. I add activeDeadlineSeconds so a stuck run is stopped. For a CronJob that misses schedules, I check whether it is suspended, the concurrencyPolicy, since Forbid skips a run if the previous one is still active, and startingDeadlineSeconds. If more than a hundred schedules are missed, the controller stops scheduling and logs an error. Clock and timezone settings also matter. To make batch reliable I make the work idempotent, set history limits and ttlSecondsAfterFinished, send alerts on failed Jobs and on a missing successful run, and make sure resources are sufficient.`,
    f: ["What does concurrencyPolicy Replace do?","How do you alert on a CronJob that never ran?"]
  }
};
